ency the outer per-entity\n     pool already had room for.\n\n5. agent_5_knowledge_graph_optimizer.py\n   - max_workers'' fallback was hardcoded to 1, fully serializing the dedup\n     batch loop and cross-batch dependency pass whenever --workers wasn''t\n     passed, despite the CLI help text and this class''s own docstring\n     describing a default of 40. Now calls config.get_max_workers().\n   - Deduplicating rules collected source_reference from every merged\n     duplicate but dropped their field_evidence (per-field citations)\n     entirely — real case on a full OPP-115 run: two sections'' only\n     citations vanished when their citing rule was merged as a duplicate,\n     failing corpus_manifest''s unexplained-removal check. Now collected and\n     merged into the surviving primary rule alongside source_reference.\n\n6. scripts/generate_benchmark_domain_prompts.py (+ the 4 domain packs it\n   generates: commercial_contracts, mobile_app_privacy, nda_confidentiality,\n   privacy_policy)\n   - The extraction prompt asked the model to emit both v1 prose\n     (conditions/consequences) and the v2 structured contract in the same\n     request — a self-contradictory instruction. Now asks only for the v2\n     contract fields, which fully supersede the v1 shape.\n\nAlso ported: two regression tests from the fork\n(test_agent_1_concurrent_organization.py,\ntest_agent_5_dedup_field_evidence.py) covering fixes #1 and #5, and the\nfork''s additions/updates to test_executable_readiness.py covering fix #4\n(including the required assertion update: the domain-agnostic BPMN gate\nmeans the existing fixture — an output variable + a responsible_party —\nnow correctly targets [\"DMN\", \"BPMN\"] instead of [\"DMN\"] alone).\n\nNot ported (out of scope for this port): the fork''s own scope-cut test\nremovals (Agent 7-10 / UI-backend / run-store tests it dropped because its\nown repo doesn''t carry those components — this repo still does and keeps\nits existing coverage for them), and its C2C_CONFIG_PATH env-var rename\n(cosmetic to that fork; this repo keeps P2K_CONFIG_PATH).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqklf3CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA5WkQAFX19hzwyuoUDdAepoYogdK0\ni4vUO1/t0ncKQCtY4fvXj43RKO6wNXOQzWMYgnSW0OKHehPSKc4MtBq9r++bDRyD\n4Ib7EdqvO36tBrEDEhZL6xRvhcywc58FkXSd9e96v5gw5kD0nlycbJKsPSNPEbpv\nqCYizlqV7L85Sx/PiJC9q1WKk+z32olabMsUO4pdrWQdlhrYRc6jsT/0YS+OgL9e\nhOUgjz+ngJlUO386vo3SZCJWcb2AePIS1pbQVruN9vNDz0xjF4NPyBneW/aE/iXN\nn2l61FtdZP9kFQuHI4TBp/TFmn72X5sJ1/7tg90qgVMZdL0YRNXKUEB/hDp2Lg6Y\nmp0ymott2W5VPz5FGI8641+1PA23Wa1j/bkkGvToeqs69m0E/BpeIXsnlaCQTmgy\nWzkLS5sXHY+dtbTAgSgtZ1x7noPaAePfRKyfbNGYa+cL4UYbxiy7v0JNAh9lh8+J\ni68ReLEJCzARdW2oR2aUaHjO9kYlg6jsNaQ01KcV60ph/2u809dasfKn6VgSlMKZ\nqj6SRTr0GfXFmcQ9OsJOVQCENUeD8jl7ImFbvNamZGXsrA4zpD8s+NkVO8Pclo+q\nhTS6z6lzsoDTB3y1ZyVJbAbQVrHvlmpeXdSM7i6ELS+Ato7URpv6M8skjgiY+8Ej\nlMArNboX6dyVpA9X9bKv\n=wSyS\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T03:54:31Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/2d87e8bfb3886b3bd2ba0d4cfc0299947a816c3b","node_id":"C_kwDOTG-nuNoAKDJkODdlOGJmYjM4ODZiM2JkMmJhMGQ0Y2ZjMDI5OTk0N2E4MTZjM2I","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/4efc9a7a6ef9e9722e0d63bf37c43c08d40dd5c4","sha":"4efc9a7a6ef9e9722e0d63bf37c43c08d40dd5c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/4efc9a7a6ef9e9722e0d63bf37c43c08d40dd5c4"}],"sha":"2d87e8bfb3886b3bd2ba0d4cfc0299947a816c3b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/2d87e8bfb3886b3bd2ba0d4cfc0299947a816c3b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/145064767?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci","id":145064767,"login":"rrahimi-uci","node_id":"U_kgDOCKWDPw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/4efc9a7a6ef9e9722e0d63bf37c43c08d40dd5c4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T03:54:12Z","email":"145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Reza Rahimi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T03:54:12Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"perf(pipeline): improve concurrent document processing and extraction robustness (#129)\n\n* perf(pipeline): process documents concurrently in Agent 1 organization\n\nAgent 1''s process_knowledge_folder chunked and organized every file in a\nstrictly single-threaded loop (plus an added time.sleep(1) between files),\neven though the underlying LLM client''s in-flight concurrency is already\ngated by KG_LLM_CONCURRENCY (default 8, set by the CLI orchestrator for\nevery agent subprocess) — the client already supports up to 8 concurrent\nreasoning calls, the loop just never issued more than one at a time.\n\nOn a real 115-document OPP-115 benchmark run this made Step 1 alone take on\nthe order of hours (~2.4 minutes/document, wholly serial) — a severity that\nwould have made the larger benchmarks in this evaluation (607-document\nContractNLI, 510-document CUAD) take roughly a day each for document\norganization alone, before any of the other 9 pipeline stages even start.\n\nEach document chunks into and writes its own isolated output subfolder, and\nthe agent holds no other mutable per-document instance state beyond the\nalready thread-safe LLM client, so dispatching every document through a\nThreadPoolExecutor (KG_ORGANIZER_WORKERS, defaulting to KG_LLM_CONCURRENCY)\nis safe. Per-file results are collected independently and merged\nafterward, preserving identical aggregate output; one file''s exception\nstill can''t corrupt another''s outcome.\n\nTests: tests/test_agent_1_concurrent_organization.py — result aggregation\ncorrectness, per-document output isolation, exception isolation, and two\ndedicated concurrency-verification tests (peak simultaneous in-flight count,\nwall-clock time against a serial baseline, and that KG_ORGANIZER_WORKERS\ncorrectly bounds the parallelism). Full suite: 1686 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): fix Agent 2 quality-analysis stub''s key-name mismatch\n\nEntityRelationshipExtractor.analyze_extraction_quality (documented as a\n\"Simple stub for meta-agent functionality\") returned\nquality_score/completeness/suggestions, but run_iterations_with_optimization\nreads overall_score/entity_quality_score/relationship_quality_score/\nbusiness_rules_score/coverage_score — every one of those .get(key, 0) reads\nsilently defaulted to 0 regardless of the stub''s actual (85) value.\n\nOn a real OPP-115 benchmark run this printed \"Overall/Entity/Relationship/\nCoverage Score: 0/100\" on every iteration despite genuine, non-empty\nextraction results (6 entities, 9-10 relationships) — cosmetically alarming\non its own — and permanently disabled the `quality_score >= quality_target`\nearly-stop path: the configured KG_ENTITY_QUALITY_TARGET (90 by default)\ncould never be satisfied by a value that was always read as 0, so only the\nnew_items diminishing-returns heuristic could ever converge a run.\n\nFixed by returning the keys the reader actually expects, preserving the\nstub''s original \"good enough, keep going\" placeholder value (85) and intent\n— business_rules_score stays 0, correctly, since business rules are Agent\n3''s output and there is genuinely nothing to score yet at this stage.\n\nTests: tests/test_review_fixes_core.py — asserts every key the convergence\ncheck reads is present, and an end-to-end case proving the quality-based\nearly-stop path can now actually fire (not just the new_items path). Full\nsuite: 1688 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): preserve field_evidence when Agent 5 merges duplicate rules\n\n_deduplicate_rules_single already collected source_reference from every\nrule in a duplicate group (primary + duplicates) into the surviving primary\nrule, but never did the same for field_evidence. A removed duplicate''s\nper-field citations (scope_basis, outcomes, exceptions, ...) are real\nevidence for the merged rule, not just its source_reference — dropping them\nsilently loses a section''s only citation whenever that section was cited\nexclusively through a duplicate''s field_evidence.\n\nReal case found on a full OPP-115 benchmark run: rule\nbatch40_lodgemfg_do_not_track_honored_no_cookies_or_ads_v1 was merged as a\nduplicate into batch63_r4_dnt_honor_..., and its field_evidence.scope_basis\nand field_evidence.outcomes citations (two distinct sections, cited nowhere\nelse in the corpus) vanished — kg_readiness.corpus_manifest''s \"every corpus\nchange needs an explicit reason\" check correctly flagged both as\nunexplained removals and hard-failed the whole run''s corpus_integrity\ninvariant, with no path to remediate it (this invariant isn''t something\nAgent 5.6 can fix after the fact — the evidence has to survive the merge\nthat happens two stages earlier).\n\nFixed by collecting field_evidence from every rule in a duplicate group the\nsame way source_reference already is: primary''s own entries first, then\neach duplicate''s, extending per field_path rather than replacing, with\nexact-duplicate entries (same section already present) not repeated.\n\nTests: tests/test_agent_5_dedup_field_evidence.py — a duplicate''s\notherwise-unique field evidence survives the merge, a field the primary\nalready has is extended not replaced, no-field_evidence-on-either-rule\ndoesn''t crash, and an already-shared citation isn''t duplicated. Full suite:\n1691 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): apply legacy value-type aliases to variables[].type too\n\nLEGACY_VALUE_TYPES/LEGACY_OPERATORS already normalise value_type fields on\ncondition_predicates/outcomes/exceptions, but variables[].type — which\nshares the exact same VARIABLE_TYPES enum — only ever got two hardcoded\nspecial cases (datetime, string) and never the alias table itself. A\nvariable typed \"string_array\"/\"enum_array\" (the model''s reasonable plural\nof an already-accepted \"list\" type, same pattern as the other aliases in\nthis table) failed v2 validation outright instead of normalising like its\nvalue_type counterparts already do.\n\nAlso adds two real-world aliases that were missing from the tables\nentirely: \"enum_array\" -> \"list\" (LEGACY_VALUE_TYPES) and \"contains_any\" ->\n\"in\" (LEGACY_OPERATORS, for the exact same \"does the actual value match any\nof a given set\" semantics \"in\" already covers).\n\nReal case from a full OPP-115 benchmark run: 5 rules failed v2 validation\nwith invalid_variable_type and 2 more with invalid_predicate_operator, all\nfrom these exact aliases — a hard schema_consistency failure with no\nremediation path (Agent 5.6 is scoped to review-required evidence gaps, not\nraw v2 structural violations), so this genuinely blocked the whole run.\n\nTests: tests/test_executable_readiness.py — variable type alias\nnormalisation, contains_any operator normalisation, and an end-to-end v2\nvalidation check. Full suite: 1694 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): fall back to a stable title when AI chunking returns blank\n\nchunk_document_with_reasoning and _build_section_path_from_ai used\nsection.get(''title'', f\"Section {i+1}\") — .get(key, default) only applies\nthe default when the key is missing, but the model sometimes returns a\npresent, blank/whitespace title (\"\" or, after _normalize_chunk_sizes joins\ntwo already-blank titles, \" + \"). That empty string then propagates all the\nway to source_reference.section_id, chunk_path/\"untitled.txt\" filenames,\nand field_evidence entries downstream.\n\nReal case from a full OPP-115 benchmark run: one document''s chunks (a\n\"miscellaneous\" trailing section with no natural heading) all got blank\ntitles, consolidated into an \"untitled.txt\" file; the rule citing them had\nsection_id: \"\" everywhere, which failed v2''s missing_evidence_reference_field\ncheck on 5 separate field_evidence entries plus missing_field_evidence on\ntwo more (the evidence backfill''s own completeness check also rejects an\nempty section_id) — a hard schema_consistency failure with no remediation\npath.\n\nFixed at the source (chunk creation) rather than patching every downstream\nreader: falls back to \"Section N\" whenever the model''s title is blank after\nstripping, not just when the key is absent. Fixing this upstream also\nresolves the \" + \" merge-artifact case, since _normalize_chunk_sizes never\nreceives a blank title to join in the first place.\n\nTests: tests/test_agent_1_concurrent_organization.py — a mocked AI response\nwith blank/whitespace-only section titles must produce stable, non-blank\nchunk titles and section paths. Full suite: 1695 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(pipeline): fix agent_3/agent_5 MAX_WORKERS fallback (1 -> 40)\n\nConfig.get_max_workers() already implements \"MAX_WORKERS env var, else\npipeline.max_workers (40)\" — the correct, documented default. Both\nagent_3_rules_extractor.py and agent_5_knowledge_graph_optimizer.py instead\nreimplemented that same lookup themselves inline, each with its own\ndifferent, wrong hardcoded fallback (20 and 1 respectively) instead of\ncalling the config helper.\n\nThe CLI intentionally leaves the MAX_WORKERS env var unset when --workers\nisn''t passed on the command line (by design — see this test file''s own\ndocstring, point 4), trusting each agent to consult the config default in\nthat case. Every run this session that didn''t explicitly pass --workers\n(which is most of them, including the two full OPP-115 attempts) silently\nran Agent 5''s entire dedup batch loop and cross-batch dependency pass\n(built on a ThreadPoolExecutor sized to self.max_workers) with exactly one\nworker — fully serial — and Agent 3''s rule-batch extraction with only 20\ninstead of the configured 40, despite config.json declaring\npipeline.max_workers: 40 and the --workers CLI help text and\nKnowledgeExtractionPipeline''s own docstring both describing that default.\n\nThis is very likely the largest single contributor to how slow the\nmulti-hour benchmark runs this session actually were, independent of the\nseparate Agent 1 document-organization concurrency fix.\n\nTests: tests/test_workers_option.py — agent_5''s optimizer now defaults its\nown max_workers to 40 (not 1) via config.get_max_workers(), picks up a\ncustom MAX_WORKERS correctly, and agent_3''s main() is confirmed (by source)\nto call the same shared config method instead of its own inline read. Full\nsuite: 1698 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): clear a document''s output folder before re-chunking it\n\norganize_chunks created a document''s output folder with mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nbut never cleared it first. Chunking is LLM-driven and non-deterministic —\na re-run of Step 1 (retry, resumed batch, manual reprocessing after a fix)\ncan produce a different set of chunk filenames than the previous run.\nWithout clearing the folder, a chunk file the new run doesn''t happen to\noverwrite is orphaned but still physically present — and every downstream\nstage globs this folder for every .txt file, so a stale chunk from a\nprevious pass is indistinguishable from current, live content.\n\nReal case: this is why the \"blank AI section title\" fix committed earlier\ntoday didn''t fully resolve on a real rerun. The fixed chunking genuinely\nstopped producing blank titles, confirmed by the freshly-written\n_metadata.json — but the previous run''s \"untitled.txt\" chunk was still\nsitting in the folder from before the fix, and Agent 3 still cited it as if\nit were current.\n\nFixed by clearing (shutil.rmtree) a document''s existing output folder\nbefore writing its new chunks, so each Step 1 pass over a document starts\nfrom a clean slate.\n\nTests: tests/test_agent_1_concurrent_organization.py — a document\nre-chunked with a different breakdown must not retain any file from the\nprior pass. Full suite: 1699 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): raise LLM client timeout from 240s to 900s\n\nopenai.rate_limiting.timeout was 240 seconds, but KG_GLOBAL_LLM_LEASE_SECONDS\n(the shared concurrency lease every request holds while in flight) is 900 —\nthe system''s own design already assumed a single request could legitimately\ntake up to 15 minutes, while the actual HTTP client timeout was less than a\nthird of that.\n\nConfirmed via a live OPP-115 benchmark run: gpt-5.2 with reasoning_effort\n\"medium\" on multi-thousand-token business-rule extraction batches regularly\ncompleted successfully in 400-600+ seconds — comfortably past the 240s\ntimeout. A direct curl check confirmed the network path to api.openai.com\nitself was fast and healthy (HTTP response in 0.13s), ruling out a real\nconnectivity problem; the repeated \"APIConnectionError\" failures this\nsession attributed to \"transient network blips\" were, at least in\nsignificant part, the client cutting off requests that were simply still\ngenerating a response.\n\nThis is worse than just failed-and-retried requests: every premature\ntimeout is misreported as a connection failure to the adaptive concurrency\nlimiter, which reacts with a multiplicative decrease exactly as if the\nprovider were failing — driving current_limit down to its floor (1) and\nrequiring a long run of consecutive successes to recover (the exact \"stuck\nat floor\" pattern diagnosed earlier this session and worked around\noperationally by resetting limiter state). Raising the timeout to 900s\n(matching the lease window) should substantially reduce how often this\nmisfires, on top of the earlier MAX_WORKERS and Agent 1 concurrency fixes.\n\nTests: tests/test_config_no_hardcoded.py updated for the new default. Full\nsuite: 1699 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(pipeline): parallelize conflict-analysis output-variable buckets\n\nanalyse_group''s large-entity branch (an entity group exceeding\nKG_CONFLICT_MAX_RULES_PER_CALL) split rules into output-variable batches\nand called the resolver for each one in a plain sequential for loop. A\nsingle dominant entity (a generic LENDER/FIRST_PARTY-style bucket) can hold\nmost of a graph''s rules, splitting into dozens of independent batches —\neach its own LLM call with no dependency on the others.\n\nConfirmed live on a real OPP-115 benchmark run: the outer per-entity\nThreadPoolExecutor and the shared LLM concurrency gate both had room for 16\nand 8 in-flight requests respectively, but this inner loop''s own\nconcurrency was fixed at 1 by construction — a live thread-count check on\nthe running process showed only the main thread active during this phase,\nand the shared limiter''s active_leases sat at 1 for the whole stretch\ndespite current_limit staying at 16.\n\nFixed by collecting all of an entity''s output-variable batches up front and\ndispatching them through a ThreadPoolExecutor sized to readiness_workers,\nsame as the outer per-entity dispatch already does.\n\nTests: tests/test_executable_readiness.py — a large entity group''s output-\nvariable buckets must run with genuine overlap (peak concurrent count > 1)\nand finish in a fraction of what serial dispatch would take. Full suite:\n1700 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"dfb1432b67441919630f48985ab1f80da7b2b444","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/dfb1432b67441919630f48985ab1f80da7b2b444"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/4efc9a7a6ef9e9722e0d63bf37c43c08d40dd5c4","verification":{"payload":"tree dfb1432b67441919630f48985ab1f80da7b2b444\nparent 58e1879bb11dfec359511298d8d1e3ab1508041b\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787975652 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787975652 -0700\n\nperf(pipeline): improve concurrent document processing and extraction robustness (#129)\n\n* perf(pipeline): process documents concurrently in Agent 1 organization\n\nAgent 1''s process_knowledge_folder chunked and organized every file in a\nstrictly single-threaded loop (plus an added time.sleep(1) between files),\neven though the underlying LLM client''s in-flight concurrency is already\ngated by KG_LLM_CONCURRENCY (default 8, set by the CLI orchestrator for\nevery agent subprocess) — the client already supports up to 8 concurrent\nreasoning calls, the loop just never issued more than one at a time.\n\nOn a real 115-document OPP-115 benchmark run this made Step 1 alone take on\nthe order of hours (~2.4 minutes/document, wholly serial) — a severity that\nwould have made the larger benchmarks in this evaluation (607-document\nContractNLI, 510-document CUAD) take roughly a day each for document\norganization alone, before any of the other 9 pipeline stages even start.\n\nEach document chunks into and writes its own isolated output subfolder, and\nthe agent holds no other mutable per-document instance state beyond the\nalready thread-safe LLM client, so dispatching every document through a\nThreadPoolExecutor (KG_ORGANIZER_WORKERS, defaulting to KG_LLM_CONCURRENCY)\nis safe. Per-file results are collected independently and merged\nafterward, preserving identical aggregate output; one file''s exception\nstill can''t corrupt another''s outcome.\n\nTests: tests/test_agent_1_concurrent_organization.py — result aggregation\ncorrectness, per-document output isolation, exception isolation, and two\ndedicated concurrency-verification tests (peak simultaneous in-flight count,\nwall-clock time against a serial baseline, and that KG_ORGANIZER_WORKERS\ncorrectly bounds the parallelism). Full suite: 1686 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): fix Agent 2 quality-analysis stub''s key-name mismatch\n\nEntityRelationshipExtractor.analyze_extraction_quality (documented as a\n\"Simple stub for meta-agent functionality\") returned\nquality_score/completeness/suggestions, but run_iterations_with_optimization\nreads overall_score/entity_quality_score/relationship_quality_score/\nbusiness_rules_score/coverage_score — every one of those .get(key, 0) reads\nsilently defaulted to 0 regardless of the stub''s actual (85) value.\n\nOn a real OPP-115 benchmark run this printed \"Overall/Entity/Relationship/\nCoverage Score: 0/100\" on every iteration despite genuine, non-empty\nextraction results (6 entities, 9-10 relationships) — cosmetically alarming\non its own — and permanently disabled the `quality_score >= quality_target`\nearly-stop path: the configured KG_ENTITY_QUALITY_TARGET (90 by default)\ncould never be satisfied by a value that was always read as 0, so only the\nnew_items diminishing-returns heuristic could ever converge a run.\n\nFixed by returning the keys the reader actually expects, preserving the\nstub''s original \"good enough, keep going\" placeholder value (85) and intent\n— business_rules_score stays 0, correctly, since business rules are Agent\n3''s output and there is genuinely nothing to score yet at this stage.\n\nTests: tests/test_review_fixes_core.py — asserts every key the convergence\ncheck reads is present, and an end-to-end case proving the quality-based\nearly-stop path can now actually fire (not just the new_items path). Full\nsuite: 1688 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): preserve field_evidence when Agent 5 merges duplicate rules\n\n_deduplicate_rules_single already collected source_reference from every\nrule in a duplicate group (primary + duplicates) into the surviving primary\nrule, but never did the same for field_evidence. A removed duplicate''s\nper-field citations (scope_basis, outcomes, exceptions, ...) are real\nevidence for the merged rule, not just its source_reference — dropping them\nsilently loses a section''s only citation whenever that section was cited\nexclusively through a duplicate''s field_evidence.\n\nReal case found on a full OPP-115 benchmark run: rule\nbatch40_lodgemfg_do_not_track_honored_no_cookies_or_ads_v1 was merged as a\nduplicate into batch63_r4_dnt_honor_..., and its field_evidence.scope_basis\nand field_evidence.outcomes citations (two distinct sections, cited nowhere\nelse in the corpus) vanished — kg_readiness.corpus_manifest''s \"every corpus\nchange needs an explicit reason\" check correctly flagged both as\nunexplained removals and hard-failed the whole run''s corpus_integrity\ninvariant, with no path to remediate it (this invariant isn''t something\nAgent 5.6 can fix after the fact — the evidence has to survive the merge\nthat happens two stages earlier).\n\nFixed by collecting field_evidence from every rule in a duplicate group the\nsame way source_reference already is: primary''s own entries first, then\neach duplicate''s, extending per field_path rather than replacing, with\nexact-duplicate entries (same section already present) not repeated.\n\nTests: tests/test_agent_5_dedup_field_evidence.py — a duplicate''s\notherwise-unique field evidence survives the merge, a field the primary\nalready has is extended not replaced, no-field_evidence-on-either-rule\ndoesn''t crash, and an already-shared citation isn''t duplicated. Full suite:\n1691 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): apply legacy value-type aliases to variables[].type too\n\nLEGACY_VALUE_TYPES/LEGACY_OPERATORS already normalise value_type fields on\ncondition_predicates/outcomes/exceptions, but variables[].type — which\nshares the exact same VARIABLE_TYPES enum — only ever got two hardcoded\nspecial cases (datetime, string) and never the alias table itself. A\nvariable typed \"string_array\"/\"enum_array\" (the model''s reasonable plural\nof an already-accepted \"list\" type, same pattern as the other aliases in\nthis table) failed v2 validation outright instead of normalising like its\nvalue_type counterparts already do.\n\nAlso adds two real-world aliases that were missing from the tables\nentirely: \"enum_array\" -> \"list\" (LEGACY_VALUE_TYPES) and \"contains_any\" ->\n\"in\" (LEGACY_OPERATORS, for the exact same \"does the actual value match any\nof a given set\" semantics \"in\" already covers).\n\nReal case from a full OPP-115 benchmark run: 5 rules failed v2 validation\nwith invalid_variable_type and 2 more with invalid_predicate_operator, all\nfrom these exact aliases — a hard schema_consistency failure with no\nremediation path (Agent 5.6 is scoped to review-required evidence gaps, not\nraw v2 structural violations), so this genuinely blocked the whole run.\n\nTests: tests/test_executable_readiness.py — variable type alias\nnormalisation, contains_any operator normalisation, and an end-to-end v2\nvalidation check. Full suite: 1694 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): fall back to a stable title when AI chunking returns blank\n\nchunk_document_with_reasoning and _build_section_path_from_ai used\nsection.get(''title'', f\"Section {i+1}\") — .get(key, default) only applies\nthe default when the key is missing, but the model sometimes returns a\npresent, blank/whitespace title (\"\" or, after _normalize_chunk_sizes joins\ntwo already-blank titles, \" + \"). That empty string then propagates all the\nway to source_reference.section_id, chunk_path/\"untitled.txt\" filenames,\nand field_evidence entries downstream.\n\nReal case from a full OPP-115 benchmark run: one document''s chunks (a\n\"miscellaneous\" trailing section with no natural heading) all got blank\ntitles, consolidated into an \"untitled.txt\" file; the rule citing them had\nsection_id: \"\" everywhere, which failed v2''s missing_evidence_reference_field\ncheck on 5 separate field_evidence entries plus missing_field_evidence on\ntwo more (the evidence backfill''s own completeness check also rejects an\nempty section_id) — a hard schema_consistency failure with no remediation\npath.\n\nFixed at the source (chunk creation) rather than patching every downstream\nreader: falls back to \"Section N\" whenever the model''s title is blank after\nstripping, not just when the key is absent. Fixing this upstream also\nresolves the \" + \" merge-artifact case, since _normalize_chunk_sizes never\nreceives a blank title to join in the first place.\n\nTests: tests/test_agent_1_concurrent_organization.py — a mocked AI response\nwith blank/whitespace-only section titles must produce stable, non-blank\nchunk titles and section paths. Full suite: 1695 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(pipeline): fix agent_3/agent_5 MAX_WORKERS fallback (1 -> 40)\n\nConfig.get_max_workers() already implements \"MAX_WORKERS env var, else\npipeline.max_workers (40)\" — the correct, documented default. Both\nagent_3_rules_extractor.py and agent_5_knowledge_graph_optimizer.py instead\nreimplemented that same lookup themselves inline, each with its own\ndifferent, wrong hardcoded fallback (20 and 1 respectively) instead of\ncalling the config helper.\n\nThe CLI intentionally leaves the MAX_WORKERS env var unset when --workers\nisn''t passed on the command line (by design — see this test file''s own\ndocstring, point 4), trusting each agent to consult the config default in\nthat case. Every run this session that didn''t explicitly pass --workers\n(which is most of them, including the two full OPP-115 attempts) silently\nran Agent 5''s entire dedup batch loop and cross-batch dependency pass\n(built on a ThreadPoolExecutor sized to self.max_workers) with exactly one\nworker — fully serial — and Agent 3''s rule-batch extraction with only 20\ninstead of the configured 40, despite config.json declaring\npipeline.max_workers: 40 and the --workers CLI help text and\nKnowledgeExtractionPipeline''s own docstring both describing that default.\n\nThis is very likely the largest single contributor to how slow the\nmulti-hour benchmark runs this session actually were, independent of the\nseparate Agent 1 document-organization concurrency fix.\n\nTests: tests/test_workers_option.py — agent_5''s optimizer now defaults its\nown max_workers to 40 (not 1) via config.get_max_workers(), picks up a\ncustom MAX_WORKERS correctly, and agent_3''s main() is confirmed (by source)\nto call the same shared config method instead of its own inline read. Full\nsuite: 1698 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): clear a document''s output folder before re-chunking it\n\norganize_chunks created a document''s output folder with mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nbut never cleared it first. Chunking is LLM-driven and non-deterministic —\na re-run of Step 1 (retry, resumed batch, manual reprocessing after a fix)\ncan produce a different set of chunk filenames than the previous run.\nWithout clearing the folder, a chunk file the new run doesn''t happen to\noverwrite is orphaned but still physically present — and every downstream\nstage globs this folder for every .txt file, so a stale chunk from a\nprevious pass is indistinguishable from current, live content.\n\nReal case: this is why the \"blank AI section title\" fix committed earlier\ntoday didn''t fully resolve on a real rerun. The fixed chunking genuinely\nstopped producing blank titles, confirmed by the freshly-written\n_metadata.json — but the previous run''s \"untitled.txt\" chunk was still\nsitting in the folder from before the fix, and Agent 3 still cited it as if\nit were current.\n\nFixed by clearing (shutil.rmtree) a document''s existing output folder\nbefore writing its new chunks, so each Step 1 pass over a document starts\nfrom a clean slate.\n\nTests: tests/test_agent_1_concurrent_organization.py — a document\nre-chunked with a different breakdown must not retain any file from the\nprior pass. Full suite: 1699 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline): raise LLM client timeout from 240s to 900s\n\nopenai.rate_limiting.timeout was 240 seconds, but KG_GLOBAL_LLM_LEASE_SECONDS\n(the shared concurrency lease every request holds while in flight) is 900 —\nthe system''s own design already assumed a single request could legitimately\ntake up to 15 minutes, while the actual HTTP client timeout was less than a\nthird of that.\n\nConfirmed via a live OPP-115 benchmark run: gpt-5.2 with reasoning_effort\n\"medium\" on multi-thousand-token business-rule extraction batches regularly\ncompleted successfully in 400-600+ seconds — comfortably past the 240s\ntimeout. A direct curl check confirmed the network path to api.openai.com\nitself was fast and healthy (HTTP response in 0.13s), ruling out a real\nconnectivity problem; the repeated \"APIConnectionError\" failures this\nsession attributed to \"transient network blips\" were, at least in\nsignificant part, the client cutting off requests that were simply still\ngenerating a response.\n\nThis is worse than just failed-and-retried requests: every premature\ntimeout is misreported as a connection failure to the adaptive concurrency\nlimiter, which reacts with a multiplicative decrease exactly as if the\nprovider were failing — driving current_limit down to its floor (1) and\nrequiring a long run of consecutive successes to recover (the exact \"stuck\nat floor\" pattern diagnosed earlier this session and worked around\noperationally by resetting limiter state). Raising the timeout to 900s\n(matching the lease window) should substantially reduce how often this\nmisfires, on top of the earlier MAX_WORKERS and Agent 1 concurrency fixes.\n\nTests: tests/test_config_no_hardcoded.py updated for the new default. Full\nsuite: 1699 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(pipeline): parallelize conflict-analysis output-variable buckets\n\nanalyse_group''s large-entity branch (an entity group exceeding\nKG_CONFLICT_MAX_RULES_PER_CALL) split rules into output-variable batches\nand called the resolver for each one in a plain sequential for loop. A\nsingle dominant entity (a generic LENDER/FIRST_PARTY-style bucket) can hold\nmost of a graph''s rules, splitting into dozens of independent batches —\neach its own LLM call with no dependency on the others.\n\nConfirmed live on a real OPP-115 benchmark run: the outer per-entity\nThreadPoolExecutor and the shared LLM concurrency gate both had room for 16\nand 8 in-flight requests respectively, but this inner loop''s own\nconcurrency was fixed at 1 by construction — a live thread-count check on\nthe running process showed only the main thread active during this phase,\nand the shared limiter''s active_leases sat at 1 for the whole stretch\ndespite current_limit staying at 16.\n\nFixed by collecting all of an entity''s output-variable batches up front and\ndispatching them through a ThreadPoolExecutor sized to readiness_workers,\nsame as the outer per-entity dispatch already does.\n\nTests: tests/test_executable_readiness.py — a large entity group''s output-\nvariable buckets must run with genuine overlap (peak concurrent count > 1)\nand finish in a fraction of what serial dispatch would take. Full suite:\n1700 passed, 0 failed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqklfkCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvkYQAIjQdlD4p7wEDt0NZGcKKqXG\nDCy+K3Oq8Cl2t2zPlaDCVqBxrHRWzLpx4LAXlprrWtQJ4XB0wO5EqML3hOa73iI7\nwq/C1k0vhKFEpNBpgWYXJBG5lFLwHgtQDISViz+ID6WwwBKn7iNXyywks4WSN3WL\n9v8IJq17n520QzCv6HhA29HSrhibNlEJy/oJ1H7lsm9L2HG6IZU8gKqntY1C+8xE\nxOXi8D4g4jOekdQL+rvoi4p1AvOVSJ+dGFzVc95S9il/vWJLJVCE6pQssUH7MJwP\npxAJ/DRExueriCsYcZ52aDeHsOUU2Xn1t0snd2suLO7+bDPHWBp+TYb3+gPj85p4\ncJaK2kx8GIZFDBIdqMEwwgRv7yl+E4FHYRv9s96cOUIa/ohqrOBwwqjl2X6b/h+F\nqqmhNZJyDBFrd0AjKa006v1dg77+ZBm8BofVkm2VAKaQn8yN/f3sSjOU9z+JOjRy\nKwAG2dY4YnW2UNMvzpBeKJVM/m+7kTv0Qge4tyYMpScYugM2AO+ouIoCJI80sfn8\ngB2b+aFfcnPVMIRjO4SR8+NwZkGKJ93Phyk/88zVuoLz8mymhckzQipK/RhkSH32\nBQSiy3tsKtXtsyM8cgU2erQ5xoVfUB32kibGQSxr/37/DTJzLzHT9RU/nbFvOpSs\nwDH+2uw+1yV37x+QS4uI\n=OagT\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T03:54:13Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/4efc9a7a6ef9e9722e0d63bf37c43c08d40dd5c4","node_id":"C_kwDOTG-nuNoAKDRlZmM5YTdhNmVmOWU5NzIyZTBkNjNiZjM3YzQzYzA4ZDQwZGQ1YzQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/58e1879bb11dfec359511298d8d1e3ab1508041b","sha":"58e1879bb11dfec359511298d8d1e3ab1508041b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/58e1879bb11dfec359511298d8d1e3ab1508041b"}],"sha":"4efc9a7a6ef9e9722e0d63bf37c43c08d40dd5c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/4efc9a7a6ef9e9722e0d63bf37c43c08d40dd5c4"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/145064767?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci","id":145064767,"login":"rrahimi-uci","node_id":"U_kgDOCKWDPw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/58e1879bb11dfec359511298d8d1e3ab1508041b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-23T21:08:51Z","email":"145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Reza Rahimi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-23T21:08:51Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs(pipeline): k-to-code plan — compiling the knowledge graph into executable DMN/BPMN (#127)\n\n* docs(pipeline): add k-to-code plan for compiling the KG into DMN/BPMN\n\nAdds apps/pipeline/k-to-code.md, a feasibility study and phased plan for\ncompiling a grounding-certified knowledge graph into executable DMN 1.3\ndecision models and BPMN 2.0 process models. Follows the style of the\nexisting complement-kg.md plan doc that produced Agents 5.5-5.7.\n\nFeasibility was measured, not assumed. A throwaway generator was run over\nthe one real v2 certified graph in the repo (352 rules,\npipeline-output/fannie_mae_readiness_20260821):\n\n- 352/352 rules emit well-formed DMN 1.3 XML; 0 generation failures\n- 0 of 1,385 predicate/outcome literals were unrenderable as FEEL\n- nested boolean logic -> DMN rows via DNF: 429 rows from 352 rules,\n  87.5% single-row, worst case 7; zero rules need negation handling\n- test-vector replay through the generated row logic: 361/384 (94.0%)\n  reproduce, and critically ZERO cases where a rule fires but yields a\n  wrong value. All 23 non-passes are accounted for (12 negative/else\n  vectors, 3 variable_reference outcomes, rest spike-check artifacts)\n\nThe plan''s core proposal is that the rules'' own source-attested\ntest_vectors become a hard publication gate: schema-validity only proves\nan engine will load the artifact, replay is what shows it decides\ncorrectly.\n\nSix blockers/preconditions are documented, four of them pre-existing\ndefects this work would otherwise silently inherit:\n\n- P1 (blocking): all five canonical committed batches are v1 prose; only\n  one dated one-off run in the repo has v2 structure at all\n- P2: agent_3''s prompt concatenates a v1-prose domain fallback with the\n  v2 contract, handing the model two conflicting schemas in one prompt\n- P3: contract_issues/requires_review on ~98% of rules are stale,\n  annotated pre-normalization and never recomputed; verified zero\n  predicates currently hold an invalid operator, and Agent 5.5''s own\n  schema_consistency invariant agrees (0 violations)\n- P4: the exceptions array''s boolean semantics (OR/AND/defeater) are\n  unspecified, and 125/352 rules have exceptions introducing variables\n  absent from condition_predicates, so the choice changes DMN columns\n- P5: only 175 of 305 DAG edges (prerequisite/sequential) are genuine\n  ordering; rendering complementary/contradictory/override as BPMN\n  sequence flows would emit a confidently wrong process model\n- P6: _project_execution gates BPMN on a hardcoded mortgage-shaped\n  rule_type set, but rule_type is unconstrained and per-domain, so five\n  of eight domains can never receive a BPMN target\n\nThe doc is explicit about the limits of the guarantee it proposes: Agent\n5.7 LLM-verifies only 6 of its 12 claim types against corpus quotes, and\nexecution/test_vector are checked structurally only. So \"certified\" does\nnot mean the DMN projection was verified against source. The correctness\nargument is the composition: 5.7 verifies conditions/outcomes against\nsource, replay verifies the emitted DMN against conditions/outcomes.\n\nAlso records the guard rail that constrains where a compiler may sit:\ntwo tests in test_inter_agent_contract_alignment.py runtime-assert\n_project_execution''s exact key set, so compilation must live strictly\ndownstream and treat execution as read-only.\n\nPlan only - no pipeline code changed. Five open questions are left\nexplicitly for decision rather than assumed (which corpora to re-run,\nexception semantics, table consolidation, DMN-vs-BPMN sequencing, and\nwhether to take a real DMN engine dependency).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(pipeline): flag the k-to-code plan''s n=1 evidence base explicitly\n\nEvery measurement in the feasibility verdict comes from a single\ndirectory - pipeline-output/fannie_mae_readiness_20260821 - because it\nis the only v2 graph that exists in the repo (blocker P1). The doc named\nthat run but never named the resulting generalization limit, which is\nthe thing a reader acting on the plan most needs to know.\n\nAdds an explicit ''evidence base is a single run (n=1)'' section\nseparating what should generalize (FEEL mappability, DNF tractability\nand DMN column derivation are schema-level properties of\nrule_contract.py, keyed off variables[].role, not domain content) from\nwhat may not (rule_type-dependent behaviour, where P6 is direct proof it\nalready breaks for five of eight domains; plus the hit-policy mix,\nconflict count, 94% replay rate, and singleton-signature distribution,\nall of which are observations about this corpus rather than invariants).\n\nProposes the cheap mitigation: re-run one non-mortgage domain - ideally\nprivacy_policy or nda_confidentiality, whose rule_type vocabularies are\nfurthest from mortgage''s - and re-run the spike against it, converting\nthe plan''s riskiest assumption into a measurement for the cost of one\nextraction run.\n\nAlso points the feasibility verdict at the caveat up front, and adds the\nn=1 exposure to the risk table as High.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"47ffcf5ea9a30fbefddfdff93c6cd8a00d51afdc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/47ffcf5ea9a30fbefddfdff93c6cd8a00d51afdc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/58e1879bb11dfec359511298d8d1e3ab1508041b","verification":{"payload":"tree 47ffcf5ea9a30fbefddfdff93c6cd8a00d51afdc\nparent 061bdd204cfc82e62398acf339b8aef89eb31bad\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787519331 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787519331 -0700\n\ndocs(pipeline): k-to-code plan — compiling the knowledge graph into executable DMN/BPMN (#127)\n\n* docs(pipeline): add k-to-code plan for compiling the KG into DMN/BPMN\n\nAdds apps/pipeline/k-to-code.md, a feasibility study and phased plan for\ncompiling a grounding-certified knowledge graph into executable DMN 1.3\ndecision models and BPMN 2.0 process models. Follows the style of the\nexisting complement-kg.md plan doc that produced Agents 5.5-5.7.\n\nFeasibility was measured, not assumed. A throwaway generator was run over\nthe one real v2 certified graph in the repo (352 rules,\npipeline-output/fannie_mae_readiness_20260821):\n\n- 352/352 rules emit well-formed DMN 1.3 XML; 0 generation failures\n- 0 of 1,385 predicate/outcome literals were unrenderable as FEEL\n- nested boolean logic -> DMN rows via DNF: 429 rows from 352 rules,\n  87.5% single-row, worst case 7; zero rules need negation handling\n- test-vector replay through the generated row logic: 361/384 (94.0%)\n  reproduce, and critically ZERO cases where a rule fires but yields a\n  wrong value. All 23 non-passes are accounted for (12 negative/else\n  vectors, 3 variable_reference outcomes, rest spike-check artifacts)\n\nThe plan''s core proposal is that the rules'' own source-attested\ntest_vectors become a hard publication gate: schema-validity only proves\nan engine will load the artifact, replay is what shows it decides\ncorrectly.\n\nSix blockers/preconditions are documented, four of them pre-existing\ndefects this work would otherwise silently inherit:\n\n- P1 (blocking): all five canonical committed batches are v1 prose; only\n  one dated one-off run in the repo has v2 structure at all\n- P2: agent_3''s prompt concatenates a v1-prose domain fallback with the\n  v2 contract, handing the model two conflicting schemas in one prompt\n- P3: contract_issues/requires_review on ~98% of rules are stale,\n  annotated pre-normalization and never recomputed; verified zero\n  predicates currently hold an invalid operator, and Agent 5.5''s own\n  schema_consistency invariant agrees (0 violations)\n- P4: the exceptions array''s boolean semantics (OR/AND/defeater) are\n  unspecified, and 125/352 rules have exceptions introducing variables\n  absent from condition_predicates, so the choice changes DMN columns\n- P5: only 175 of 305 DAG edges (prerequisite/sequential) are genuine\n  ordering; rendering complementary/contradictory/override as BPMN\n  sequence flows would emit a confidently wrong process model\n- P6: _project_execution gates BPMN on a hardcoded mortgage-shaped\n  rule_type set, but rule_type is unconstrained and per-domain, so five\n  of eight domains can never receive a BPMN target\n\nThe doc is explicit about the limits of the guarantee it proposes: Agent\n5.7 LLM-verifies only 6 of its 12 claim types against corpus quotes, and\nexecution/test_vector are checked structurally only. So \"certified\" does\nnot mean the DMN projection was verified against source. The correctness\nargument is the composition: 5.7 verifies conditions/outcomes against\nsource, replay verifies the emitted DMN against conditions/outcomes.\n\nAlso records the guard rail that constrains where a compiler may sit:\ntwo tests in test_inter_agent_contract_alignment.py runtime-assert\n_project_execution''s exact key set, so compilation must live strictly\ndownstream and treat execution as read-only.\n\nPlan only - no pipeline code changed. Five open questions are left\nexplicitly for decision rather than assumed (which corpora to re-run,\nexception semantics, table consolidation, DMN-vs-BPMN sequencing, and\nwhether to take a real DMN engine dependency).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(pipeline): flag the k-to-code plan''s n=1 evidence base explicitly\n\nEvery measurement in the feasibility verdict comes from a single\ndirectory - pipeline-output/fannie_mae_readiness_20260821 - because it\nis the only v2 graph that exists in the repo (blocker P1). The doc named\nthat run but never named the resulting generalization limit, which is\nthe thing a reader acting on the plan most needs to know.\n\nAdds an explicit ''evidence base is a single run (n=1)'' section\nseparating what should generalize (FEEL mappability, DNF tractability\nand DMN column derivation are schema-level properties of\nrule_contract.py, keyed off variables[].role, not domain content) from\nwhat may not (rule_type-dependent behaviour, where P6 is direct proof it\nalready breaks for five of eight domains; plus the hit-policy mix,\nconflict count, 94% replay rate, and singleton-signature distribution,\nall of which are observations about this corpus rather than invariants).\n\nProposes the cheap mitigation: re-run one non-mortgage domain - ideally\nprivacy_policy or nda_confidentiality, whose rule_type vocabularies are\nfurthest from mortgage''s - and re-run the spike against it, converting\nthe plan''s riskiest assumption into a measurement for the cost of one\nextraction run.\n\nAlso points the feasibility verdict at the caveat up front, and adds the\nn=1 exposure to the risk table as High.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqi2FjCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA2UMQALFu73qLpC9/BUYkY/jru4Zv\nxG/vFKx7O8Vg1nTW/lsa6pY+CabfvxxABi4i4WcTXm87LhFxDwLg5tbqyKHL2zqw\nXTh7EiOTyo0orVpNfvkcz7bS8UW7HSyiuuVEhlgOoUXTkr8/eysyWwwUNmnu46I2\ncLgBI9N3+YdMwZU9ncXUYiCYpEW9/bU6yhku6gxmpUcaaOYYO6fyecI+/5DvB1IG\newrhN/4tEatJUgCyWY+KBpAhCt2aK8k3t89YFoU0ByO9uV5/EpBPndu4NQie8gFr\nV7Fh0iIPDNJU0S5XLJ5vZ48DA+mttSvwYMJ3+segjlJFRLA30I6W9jUpj2bDSLU7\n61WyXEOoifIesf4OW0M0VJEVXJzbPJ2expZDMZ0hwhlMOJb/lAuO33e6aWS3d1Y/\nzMdx5hN3yAixQ4yIqK29jzrqYZM6x/5H6AR5WyEI5vEv40dUGcW9J0JCkmpDgErI\nQXwsC+utWrYxetid4W8R64Jjhpy5g8mto3HhRG3uKHXRsuyB/SDDDTaAQHY+5/4R\nXqoU3eZxT/hdMOtBqZ10E1lsQcv1O+c0zKpMKQ5Cpne32YYaibIojXxf6n9HD+57\nzcMbZNQ7VdiKKnprW7byK1BSham0QFwUtzkytjFpDJ8P5U0xpo7cl9IdoFb6henn\ng3KBvyuCHWCoNkIMN6Ej\n=yxVv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-23T21:08:51Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/58e1879bb11dfec359511298d8d1e3ab1508041b","node_id":"C_kwDOTG-nuNoAKDU4ZTE4NzliYjExZGZlYzM1OTUxMTI5OGQ4ZDFlM2FiMTUwODA0MWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/061bdd204cfc82e62398acf339b8aef89eb31bad","sha":"061bdd204cfc82e62398acf339b8aef89eb31bad","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/061bdd204cfc82e62398acf339b8aef89eb31bad"}],"sha":"58e1879bb11dfec359511298d8d1e3ab1508041b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/58e1879bb11dfec359511298d8d1e3ab1508041b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/145064767?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci","id":145064767,"login":"rrahimi-uci","node_id":"U_kgDOCKWDPw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/061bdd204cfc82e62398acf339b8aef89eb31bad/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-23T18:34:36Z","email":"145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Reza Rahimi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-23T18:34:36Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(pipeline): insert stage-6 dependency-DAG generation, shift visualization to stage 7 (#126)\n\nNew stage 6 - utils/dag_builder.py + agents/agent_6_dag_generator.py\n\nPartitions every rule in the optimized knowledge graph into one or more\ndirected acyclic graphs built from the dependency edges Agent 5 already\ncomputed (utils/kg_readiness.py:dependency_edges). This is a structural\n100%-coverage guarantee, not a best-effort pass:\n\n- Every rule is assigned to exactly one weakly-connected dependency\n  component via union-find; a rule with zero dependencies becomes its\n  own single-node DAG. The union of every generated DAG''s rule_ids is\n  therefore always the full rule set by construction.\n- A dependency cycle (detected per-component via Kosaraju''s algorithm,\n  hand-rolled - no new graph-library dependency, matching the existing\n  style of utils/kg_readiness.py) is condensed into a single\n  \"cycle_group\" node rather than dropped, so the emitted DAG stays\n  acyclic (SCC-condensation of any directed graph is itself a DAG)\n  without ever excluding a rule that participates in a cycle.\n- The coverage check is re-verified explicitly in the result (not just\n  assumed from the algorithm), and both agent_6_dag_generator.py and\n  cli/extract.py''s step6_generate_dag() fail the run (nonzero exit) if\n  it is ever not 100% - this should be unreachable, but isn''t trusted\n  blindly.\n\nVerified against all 4 canonical committed pipeline-output batches\n(fannie_mae, freddie_mac, healthcare, comercial_lending): 100% coverage\non every one, including 4 real dependency cycles found and correctly\ncondensed (fannie_mae x2, healthcare x1, comercial_lending x1) without\nlosing any rule. utils/dag_builder.py has its own 16-test unit suite\n(coverage guarantee, cycle handling incl. cycles with up/downstream\nrules, self-loops, dangling edges, determinism, and a 25-trial randomized\nstress test) independent of the CLI wiring.\n\nRenumbered stage 6 -> 7 (agent_6_visualization_and_report.py)\n\nKept the script''s filename and output directory\n(agent-6-visualization-and-report/) unchanged - only cli/extract.py''s\n--step value / progress banners shift to 7. The alternative (renaming\nthe file and directory to match) would have required git-mv''ing that\ndirectory inside 5 already-committed canonical pipeline-output batches\nfor a purely cosmetic rename; not worth the churn. Documented this\nexplicitly in utils/config.py''s get_visualization_dir() docstring and\nin agents/README.md''s new numbering note, including why extraction''s\nnew step 7 and comparison''s independent Agent 7\n(agent_7_rule_type_clusterer.py, cli/compare.py) are unrelated despite\nsharing a number.\n\nWiring updated end-to-end: cli/extract.py (step dispatch, argparse\nchoices, run_complete_flow banners/total_steps, progress summary),\nui/backend/services/pipeline_runner.py (STEP_LABELS, steps_to_track,\nfinal_step), and the 4 frontend files that had their own copies of the\nstep list (WorkflowDiagram.tsx, ProgressTracker.tsx, Pipeline.tsx,\nusePipeline.ts) - including fixing a same-id-different-meaning\ncollision in WorkflowDiagram.tsx''s phaseLink() and avoiding introducing\none in ProgressTracker.tsx''s shared STEP_LABELS map (that component is\ncompare.py-only in practice, so its ''7'' must stay the comparison-flow\nmeaning).\n\nTesting:\n- utils/dag_builder.py: 16 new unit tests (tests/test_dag_builder.py).\n- Full apps/pipeline suite: 1697 passed (was 1681 on main before this\n  change; +16 for the new test file).\n- Manual end-to-end smoke test of cli/extract.py --step 6 and --step 7\n  against a scratch copy of real freddie_mac output - correct\n  directories, correct STEP N banners, correct exit codes.\n- npx tsc -b --force on ui/frontend: clean, no errors.\n- git diff --check: clean.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"41adba3595ed4be7829add95589e9dd0224fb8f2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/41adba3595ed4be7829add95589e9dd0224fb8f2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/061bdd204cfc82e62398acf339b8aef89eb31bad","verification":{"payload":"tree 41adba3595ed4be7829add95589e9dd0224fb8f2\nparent 35b8ef172607489536935a0dbfde2937691e7a84\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787510076 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787510076 -0700\n\nfeat(pipeline): insert stage-6 dependency-DAG generation, shift visualization to stage 7 (#126)\n\nNew stage 6 - utils/dag_builder.py + agents/agent_6_dag_generator.py\n\nPartitions every rule in the optimized knowledge graph into one or more\ndirected acyclic graphs built from the dependency edges Agent 5 already\ncomputed (utils/kg_readiness.py:dependency_edges). This is a structural\n100%-coverage guarantee, not a best-effort pass:\n\n- Every rule is assigned to exactly one weakly-connected dependency\n  component via union-find; a rule with zero dependencies becomes its\n  own single-node DAG. The union of every generated DAG''s rule_ids is\n  therefore always the full rule set by construction.\n- A dependency cycle (detected per-component via Kosaraju''s algorithm,\n  hand-rolled - no new graph-library dependency, matching the existing\n  style of utils/kg_readiness.py) is condensed into a single\n  \"cycle_group\" node rather than dropped, so the emitted DAG stays\n  acyclic (SCC-condensation of any directed graph is itself a DAG)\n  without ever excluding a rule that participates in a cycle.\n- The coverage check is re-verified explicitly in the result (not just\n  assumed from the algorithm), and both agent_6_dag_generator.py and\n  cli/extract.py''s step6_generate_dag() fail the run (nonzero exit) if\n  it is ever not 100% - this should be unreachable, but isn''t trusted\n  blindly.\n\nVerified against all 4 canonical committed pipeline-output batches\n(fannie_mae, freddie_mac, healthcare, comercial_lending): 100% coverage\non every one, including 4 real dependency cycles found and correctly\ncondensed (fannie_mae x2, healthcare x1, comercial_lending x1) without\nlosing any rule. utils/dag_builder.py has its own 16-test unit suite\n(coverage guarantee, cycle handling incl. cycles with up/downstream\nrules, self-loops, dangling edges, determinism, and a 25-trial randomized\nstress test) independent of the CLI wiring.\n\nRenumbered stage 6 -> 7 (agent_6_visualization_and_report.py)\n\nKept the script''s filename and output directory\n(agent-6-visualization-and-report/) unchanged - only cli/extract.py''s\n--step value / progress banners shift to 7. The alternative (renaming\nthe file and directory to match) would have required git-mv''ing that\ndirectory inside 5 already-committed canonical pipeline-output batches\nfor a purely cosmetic rename; not worth the churn. Documented this\nexplicitly in utils/config.py''s get_visualization_dir() docstring and\nin agents/README.md''s new numbering note, including why extraction''s\nnew step 7 and comparison''s independent Agent 7\n(agent_7_rule_type_clusterer.py, cli/compare.py) are unrelated despite\nsharing a number.\n\nWiring updated end-to-end: cli/extract.py (step dispatch, argparse\nchoices, run_complete_flow banners/total_steps, progress summary),\nui/backend/services/pipeline_runner.py (STEP_LABELS, steps_to_track,\nfinal_step), and the 4 frontend files that had their own copies of the\nstep list (WorkflowDiagram.tsx, ProgressTracker.tsx, Pipeline.tsx,\nusePipeline.ts) - including fixing a same-id-different-meaning\ncollision in WorkflowDiagram.tsx''s phaseLink() and avoiding introducing\none in ProgressTracker.tsx''s shared STEP_LABELS map (that component is\ncompare.py-only in practice, so its ''7'' must stay the comparison-flow\nmeaning).\n\nTesting:\n- utils/dag_builder.py: 16 new unit tests (tests/test_dag_builder.py).\n- Full apps/pipeline suite: 1697 passed (was 1681 on main before this\n  change; +16 for the new test file).\n- Manual end-to-end smoke test of cli/extract.py --step 6 and --step 7\n  against a scratch copy of real freddie_mac output - correct\n  directories, correct STEP N banners, correct exit codes.\n- npx tsc -b --force on ui/frontend: clean, no errors.\n- git diff --check: clean.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqiz08CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAdWUQAFB2qJ/ec2lnQAgRP+5JfoWQ\nkxX6ETDkLv4fyjoP9dPh6KHPBixFOenNd95S14Z1RhcRoY4ReMGDk8+tCzQItc1N\nNkiNxzm6/2scnOjMKprR/Duih9tq+pfixgtDB1YEt4Wnig1HEumVNaoxZ7RwxZMk\nMkwibRjCOTkXc/5O5m3FULdfYGhHaIklyUcCGpI1//iAZAF9yJIdmZIwlRFqZw0+\nVtqj/iVvH/CpnNYCfzd19tu/6drHKpBW5S3clkMk6tJVTDGIjej/LQewrQ02Gb2f\nPwWdZ3lYwohx6hXewXdA+P7oCsZqhdBq8ZTOj6c/TIPeR7HYNUsHRPA4/L6sFCz4\n/8KiR4BiyWIuFU+HtcqjRWeEXKpbylGfCIszoWwrCdKbL0skMdShejAfND1TkhMm\n9pVbZM5PVQb/2O/fWpzlaOy3FcQCVkMBP7uy4L8OoY2G/6UeCABqos2kCLWvn0tw\nk7SZy7dLxdWKWr+saVyCs36q6Ujm82F9YeYyUTdM6W6s9XQDtvNvceuse/CNWqrg\nwZiygaMKXQXDcC/Z3MeQqEHa/aJ9PGwU9d84ZEg2ikXrLweMef2SNh0STv7q4kbU\nikpiNXYLR8UNynSKfTcRb3ZAQAcGGDaj87TRICOCIY9XCRs1oPNIZaI8t5PkpJac\nFAs7SfSIKHV46qT9Ja+Z\n=Cu0G\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-23T18:34:36Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/061bdd204cfc82e62398acf339b8aef89eb31bad","node_id":"C_kwDOTG-nuNoAKDA2MWJkZDIwNGNmYzgyZTYyMzk4YWNmMzM5YjhhZWY4OWViMzFiYWQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/35b8ef172607489536935a0dbfde2937691e7a84","sha":"35b8ef172607489536935a0dbfde2937691e7a84","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/35b8ef172607489536935a0dbfde2937691e7a84"}],"sha":"061bdd204cfc82e62398acf339b8aef89eb31bad","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/061bdd204cfc82e62398acf339b8aef89eb31bad"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/145064767?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci","id":145064767,"login":"rrahimi-uci","node_id":"U_kgDOCKWDPw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/35b8ef172607489536935a0dbfde2937691e7a84/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-23T17:26:53Z","email":"145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Reza Rahimi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-23T17:26:53Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore(pipeline,benchmarks): gitignore ad-hoc pipeline-output/benchmark runs (#125)\n\n''apps/pipeline/pipeline-output/'' intentionally commits five canonical\nexample batches (fannie_mae, freddie_mac, healthcare, comercial_lending,\n_merged) so a fresh clone has real, populated graphs — but nothing\nenforced that boundary. A local pipeline run or benchmark pilot under\nthe same directory showed up as ~1,540 untracked files with no\n.gitignore rule catching them:\n\n- apps/pipeline/pipeline-output/contract-nli-pilot-docs/\n- apps/pipeline/pipeline-output/fannie_mae_readiness_20260822/\n- apps/pipeline/pipeline-output/opp-115-source-docs/\n- apps/benchmarks/contract-nli-pilot-docs/\n\n(A fourth, fannie_mae_readiness_20260821/, was already hidden via a\nmachine-local .git/info/exclude entry — evidence this had already come\nup before, just not fixed for anyone else''s clone.)\n\napps/pipeline/.gitignore had a commented-out ''pipeline-output/**'' rule\nwith a note to enable it, but blanket-ignoring the directory would have\nun-tracked the 10,079 files in the five canonical batches. Replaced it\nwith a targeted default-ignore + explicit re-include of just those five\ndirectories, so new ad-hoc/dated pipeline-output runs are ignored by\ndefault while the canonical batches (and any future files added inside\nthem) stay tracked exactly as before.\n\napps/benchmarks/.gitignore already ignored ''*-source-docs/'' as corpus\noutput that''s ''downloaded, not vendored, reproducible via script'' —\nadded the same treatment for ''*-pilot-docs/'', the same kind of derived,\nhand-curated corpus subset.\n\nVerified with ''git check-ignore'': all four target directories are now\nignored, the five canonical batches and new files added inside them are\nnot, and ''git status'' is clean of these ad-hoc runs.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"e48bb5f2efb97496449a63acfeed1f774f3f8906","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/e48bb5f2efb97496449a63acfeed1f774f3f8906"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/35b8ef172607489536935a0dbfde2937691e7a84","verification":{"payload":"tree e48bb5f2efb97496449a63acfeed1f774f3f8906\nparent 490755e49ed1b20af4ca228a9a85661561555829\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787506013 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787506013 -0700\n\nchore(pipeline,benchmarks): gitignore ad-hoc pipeline-output/benchmark runs (#125)\n\n''apps/pipeline/pipeline-output/'' intentionally commits five canonical\nexample batches (fannie_mae, freddie_mac, healthcare, comercial_lending,\n_merged) so a fresh clone has real, populated graphs — but nothing\nenforced that boundary. A local pipeline run or benchmark pilot under\nthe same directory showed up as ~1,540 untracked files with no\n.gitignore rule catching them:\n\n- apps/pipeline/pipeline-output/contract-nli-pilot-docs/\n- apps/pipeline/pipeline-output/fannie_mae_readiness_20260822/\n- apps/pipeline/pipeline-output/opp-115-source-docs/\n- apps/benchmarks/contract-nli-pilot-docs/\n\n(A fourth, fannie_mae_readiness_20260821/, was already hidden via a\nmachine-local .git/info/exclude entry — evidence this had already come\nup before, just not fixed for anyone else''s clone.)\n\napps/pipeline/.gitignore had a commented-out ''pipeline-output/**'' rule\nwith a note to enable it, but blanket-ignoring the directory would have\nun-tracked the 10,079 files in the five canonical batches. Replaced it\nwith a targeted default-ignore + explicit re-include of just those five\ndirectories, so new ad-hoc/dated pipeline-output runs are ignored by\ndefault while the canonical batches (and any future files added inside\nthem) stay tracked exactly as before.\n\napps/benchmarks/.gitignore already ignored ''*-source-docs/'' as corpus\noutput that''s ''downloaded, not vendored, reproducible via script'' —\nadded the same treatment for ''*-pilot-docs/'', the same kind of derived,\nhand-curated corpus subset.\n\nVerified with ''git check-ignore'': all four target directories are now\nignored, the five canonical batches and new files added inside them are\nnot, and ''git status'' is clean of these ad-hoc runs.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqiy1dCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA71kQAF6k7yzylH9e3/bm/kJTfr//\nqIEUR/m3Kt7trE52CjW0fEXBijwuHpCFP5JWWgJQ28CkXnslVjBWYjga38Q3+HcH\nKK/kmgOuhNBcwbzkDMBmy36Dqb5nBDp+vN8vkRRdsNfBfPJiH7LYveDdDgKNhFui\nd2KZXyymPI1DPzl+BsWIhjjOWQsbgvR7Ih0GKKSA/XHRs6GTpepm34LpMANM1mQh\nKMtjjTcOSnlv0iV/3eZ8i65fLAqCd6eM6JwvreDsccjsA19kdJ+H/D7IsOsgXUA/\ntovCbQeWPwa7jrXb+fHoWprEGngrM7ly2vvRvkx56EejyWtUtHDhZLuaUa+pb4x9\nNrJ/YH4a3d1zH8N3ZqIbc8U4ks2UHgxr7XL4QkyGpDEq/W4VVLcFw4oO8hcf7qVo\n8YZunXVUPOZQSTu0ZddPh0s6kniPE0eBVapZgMsgggriJJzXff7LqVwSjnLMlOkN\n4ek4pB5vTXLtSVSl3pfJowqOc2VqXElGRMF4HoiGgeVrPDp4pmu8wCUFY1Mvzr3R\nAjhFgb7J7Xgv5RX1dW24ijPBrHNvGBMQrg5OQOavimW+9mIUAI+xiVizulPqqybA\n+GglXvVOjFAZOYRIrI8F7fIuNZFLSvEQ0F4iH8DBXO/PZ/53z0rOrJHx2OJsWhXW\nubmitq2zJWTtSMgpumu8\n=7KaN\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-23T17:26:53Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/35b8ef172607489536935a0dbfde2937691e7a84","node_id":"C_kwDOTG-nuNoAKDM1YjhlZjE3MjYwNzQ4OTUzNjkzNWEwZGJmZGUyOTM3NjkxZTdhODQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/490755e49ed1b20af4ca228a9a85661561555829","sha":"490755e49ed1b20af4ca228a9a85661561555829","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/490755e49ed1b20af4ca228a9a85661561555829"}],"sha":"35b8ef172607489536935a0dbfde2937691e7a84","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/35b8ef172607489536935a0dbfde2937691e7a84"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/145064767?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci","id":145064767,"login":"rrahimi-uci","node_id":"U_kgDOCKWDPw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/490755e49ed1b20af4ca228a9a85661561555829/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-23T17:18:54Z","email":"145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Reza Rahimi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-23T17:18:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"perf(pipeline): raise LLM concurrency defaults to unblock parallelism (#124)\n\nThe pipeline''s real throughput ceiling was not the per-agent thread\npools (max_workers=40, matcher/join workers=30/31) but two much\nsmaller knobs those threads all funnel through:\n\n- The shared adaptive rate limiter (utils/adaptive_limiter.py),\n  process-shared via SQLite, capped total in-flight OpenAI requests\n  across every agent subprocess in a run at 16, starting at 8 and\n  ramping by +1 only after 4 consecutive successes.\n- Several agents (document organizer, entity extractor, rule\n  validator, KG optimizer, semantic matcher) construct their\n  LLMClient without an explicit concurrency=, so they fall back to\n  the KG_LLM_CONCURRENCY env default of 8 as their own per-process\n  gate — a second, tighter ceiling in front of the shared limiter.\n\nNet effect: no matter how many local worker threads or concurrent\ndocument subprocesses were configured, actual API concurrency for a\nrun was bounded at 8-16 in-flight requests. For GPT-5.2 reasoning\ncalls that individually run tens of seconds, that ceiling — not\nthread-pool size — was the dominant driver of end-to-end wall clock.\n\nRaise the defaults so the already-built parallelism (worker pools,\ndocument-level concurrency, the conflict-analysis output-variable\nbucketing from 14f4e8ae) actually gets used:\n\n- pipeline.document_workers: 4 -> 6\n- pipeline.performance.global_llm_concurrency_initial: 8 -> 12\n- pipeline.performance.global_llm_concurrency_max: 16 -> 32\n- pipeline.performance.global_llm_success_window: 4 -> 3 (ramp faster)\n- pipeline.performance.llm_concurrency: 8 -> 16\n- pipeline.performance.readiness_workers: 16 -> 40 (match remediation/grounding)\n- pipeline.performance.readiness_llm_concurrency: 8 -> 16\n- pipeline.performance.remediation_llm_concurrency: 8 -> 16\n- pipeline.performance.grounding_llm_concurrency: 16 -> 24\n\nThe adaptive limiter is self-correcting by design (halves its\ncurrent_limit and backs off on the first 429/timeout), so a higher\nceiling only changes behavior when the account''s real rate limit\nsupports it; a lower actual limit is rediscovered automatically\nrather than causing sustained failures.\n\nUpdated the pinned-default assertions in\ntests/test_config_no_hardcoded.py and the throughput/tuning\ndocumentation in README.md to match. config.json (local, gitignored)\nwas updated the same way but isn''t part of this diff.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b1854736ace377dfc224bdce8abaa6a9ad74fae5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/b1854736ace377dfc224bdce8abaa6a9ad74fae5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/490755e49ed1b20af4ca228a9a85661561555829","verification":{"payload":"tree b1854736ace377dfc224bdce8abaa6a9ad74fae5\nparent 9eba8c217bb6fbba72c4b9fb6dd43e74b14c465f\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787505534 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787505534 -0700\n\nperf(pipeline): raise LLM concurrency defaults to unblock parallelism (#124)\n\nThe pipeline''s real throughput ceiling was not the per-agent thread\npools (max_workers=40, matcher/join workers=30/31) but two much\nsmaller knobs those threads all funnel through:\n\n- The shared adaptive rate limiter (utils/adaptive_limiter.py),\n  process-shared via SQLite, capped total in-flight OpenAI requests\n  across every agent subprocess in a run at 16, starting at 8 and\n  ramping by +1 only after 4 consecutive successes.\n- Several agents (document organizer, entity extractor, rule\n  validator, KG optimizer, semantic matcher) construct their\n  LLMClient without an explicit concurrency=, so they fall back to\n  the KG_LLM_CONCURRENCY env default of 8 as their own per-process\n  gate — a second, tighter ceiling in front of the shared limiter.\n\nNet effect: no matter how many local worker threads or concurrent\ndocument subprocesses were configured, actual API concurrency for a\nrun was bounded at 8-16 in-flight requests. For GPT-5.2 reasoning\ncalls that individually run tens of seconds, that ceiling — not\nthread-pool size — was the dominant driver of end-to-end wall clock.\n\nRaise the defaults so the already-built parallelism (worker pools,\ndocument-level concurrency, the conflict-analysis output-variable\nbucketing from 14f4e8ae) actually gets used:\n\n- pipeline.document_workers: 4 -> 6\n- pipeline.performance.global_llm_concurrency_initial: 8 -> 12\n- pipeline.performance.global_llm_concurrency_max: 16 -> 32\n- pipeline.performance.global_llm_success_window: 4 -> 3 (ramp faster)\n- pipeline.performance.llm_concurrency: 8 -> 16\n- pipeline.performance.readiness_workers: 16 -> 40 (match remediation/grounding)\n- pipeline.performance.readiness_llm_concurrency: 8 -> 16\n- pipeline.performance.remediation_llm_concurrency: 8 -> 16\n- pipeline.performance.grounding_llm_concurrency: 16 -> 24\n\nThe adaptive limiter is self-correcting by design (halves its\ncurrent_limit and backs off on the first 429/timeout), so a higher\nceiling only changes behavior when the account''s real rate limit\nsupports it; a lower actual limit is rediscovered automatically\nrather than causing sustained failures.\n\nUpdated the pinned-default assertions in\ntests/test_config_no_hardcoded.py and the throughput/tuning\ndocumentation in README.md to match. config.json (local, gitignored)\nwas updated the same way but isn''t part of this diff.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqiyt+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA+UcQAG/hGs+Wq23pit+DisD1izeN\nkWV/v8I5do9o27rOVY9hsXHr12O+cBFojLVz2O1VwxJnj9RA+eKgMLSCSoeh7UjA\nD2GPGn450r/pnt9yyO/LOxs2TImpQyp4Il7w0wtYMu6EcUbX/8vtjoSQOMpyGPef\nSVV9rc065G9gBjViLSDno/Bh5DCeMQAROxMOKeZPr2kfzuNyQHg+hUPtV9/QWs+k\nt0gnqX4fXI4Fk2HX1yenkawVbwKPVhMUfhfYK5cisvm3rkZgyO3nzmug2jRf73Sr\nBOTVh3KTWNR2vLptHI1Z7CMAMRhLaMJ/06KVdt6jYA22WE2gPJ66lDPiYdE5kBJC\nAqmVzF/FVSH+w2xXwgSSnAyDOiI7IeXwG/2hLRCGtjV3dmMhrzRRcMli9F1NJizg\njf8nuVI2yrX9TVroa3x5/O9lghksgBwqw8waIkL5xbmqt2GvsJL9YMkzGhUI/dt0\n4/V0hdx5v3d0E79h1uIDgPNCtuS0Mkyvply4Zva/m2Qr6CW7XwEC7rh3GyTBaFtm\n/9T0IjoiM5ggndIK9EQ7TOo1+m6eEM6BWY8cIK+IqPT15wuFbqTzZquW4qcvyFIF\nWZn07HTQ9LxSubvkHLM3t7soVab+wmj03p88YPzNNLElZQETjNFL1WYH7ZC2zDs8\nB8CIGFqsO04MvUxP6U3a\n=IekB\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-23T17:18:54Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/490755e49ed1b20af4ca228a9a85661561555829","node_id":"C_kwDOTG-nuNoAKDQ5MDc1NWU0OWVkMWIyMGFmNGNhMjI4YTlhODU2NjE1NjE1NTU4Mjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/9eba8c217bb6fbba72c4b9fb6dd43e74b14c465f","sha":"9eba8c217bb6fbba72c4b9fb6dd43e74b14c465f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/9eba8c217bb6fbba72c4b9fb6dd43e74b14c465f"}],"sha":"490755e49ed1b20af4ca228a9a85661561555829","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/490755e49ed1b20af4ca228a9a85661561555829"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/145064767?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci","id":145064767,"login":"rrahimi-uci","node_id":"U_kgDOCKWDPw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/9eba8c217bb6fbba72c4b9fb6dd43e74b14c465f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-22T19:00:46Z","email":"145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Reza Rahimi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-22T19:00:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs: retire the pipeline-p2c references left by its removal (#121)\n\nPR #120 removed apps/pipeline-p2c but deliberately left the two research plans\nthat referenced it, since rewriting a research premise is an authorial call\nrather than a mechanical edit. This makes those edits.\n\nneurips-2027-execution-plan.md treated pipeline-p2c as its candidate system.\nAdds an implementation-status note recording that no in-tree implementation of\nevidence-gated compilation remains, and rewrites the two rows of the evidence\nboundary that described the compiler:\n\n- Pipeline: states the compiler was removed and that restoring or rebuilding it\n  is a precondition of Gate 0, not a step within it.\n- Validation: states the fail-closed admission gate went with it, and names\n  what apps/pipeline still does (agent_3_5_rule_validator, agent_5_5_executable\n  _readiness, utils/kg_readiness) without calling any of it a gate.\n\npipeline-knowledge-graph-reextraction-plan.md scoped itself as \"does not change\napps/pipeline-p2c\", which is now vacuous; the sentence now points at the\nNeurIPS plan for the consequence.\n\nLeft alone: the p2c-direct-policy-qa-v1 and p2c-policy-ir-* strings in\npolicy-ir-query-evaluation-protocol.md. Those are prompt version identifiers,\nnot references to the app, and renaming them would break the protocol''s ids.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"61cfdd741acc78cab04778bbb5721e035e74f57c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/61cfdd741acc78cab04778bbb5721e035e74f57c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/9eba8c217bb6fbba72c4b9fb6dd43e74b14c465f","verification":{"payload":"tree 61cfdd741acc78cab04778bbb5721e035e74f57c\nparent 9594703b81012a62ae8588f8db167d7ce7141146\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787425246 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787425246 -0700\n\ndocs: retire the pipeline-p2c references left by its removal (#121)\n\nPR #120 removed apps/pipeline-p2c but deliberately left the two research plans\nthat referenced it, since rewriting a research premise is an authorial call\nrather than a mechanical edit. This makes those edits.\n\nneurips-2027-execution-plan.md treated pipeline-p2c as its candidate system.\nAdds an implementation-status note recording that no in-tree implementation of\nevidence-gated compilation remains, and rewrites the two rows of the evidence\nboundary that described the compiler:\n\n- Pipeline: states the compiler was removed and that restoring or rebuilding it\n  is a precondition of Gate 0, not a step within it.\n- Validation: states the fail-closed admission gate went with it, and names\n  what apps/pipeline still does (agent_3_5_rule_validator, agent_5_5_executable\n  _readiness, utils/kg_readiness) without calling any of it a gate.\n\npipeline-knowledge-graph-reextraction-plan.md scoped itself as \"does not change\napps/pipeline-p2c\", which is now vacuous; the sentence now points at the\nNeurIPS plan for the consequence.\n\nLeft alone: the p2c-direct-policy-qa-v1 and p2c-policy-ir-* strings in\npolicy-ir-query-evaluation-protocol.md. Those are prompt version identifiers,\nnot references to the app, and renaming them would break the protocol''s ids.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqifHeCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA8aMQADMb6EmZakCyK2fEoB5yiEcf\n9J833w1z+hj12PUZMo48eM+iaJ5KuKbqw9o69tR8023y+rivTVnGqLEEaYjWtAqC\n0p0wZ+FN97hC/KfRjixb3i/e957RqMNDgpugvErsXQMGBy3tTU2gKUdJF04qjSjz\nR7qnfBH7k2Hl7Mz3cNzKK1KL7NQS7l/jp6D4YLUP+A2mCIZ2MUv1NxfyGJEsJNBM\nS9NkuOYbqMCFdbTAC