_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci","id":145064767,"login":"rrahimi-uci","node_id":"U_kgDOCKWDPw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/918c841a0d9d204ce65f2461dd076bb0e368a436/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T18:11:57Z","email":"145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Reza Rahimi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T18:11:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(pipeline-p2c): add the extraction contract and a model-free baseline (#97)\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): add the extraction contract and a model-free baseline\n\nCloses the last deterministic gap: documents now reach the knowledge graph\nend to end without a model anywhere in the path.\n\nextraction/sentences.py segments canonical text while preserving absolute\noffsets, because those offsets are the provenance a clause will cite — a\nsegmenter returning strings would force a search to recover position, and\nboilerplate that recurs verbatim could then not be located at all.\nSplitting is conservative: \"Ch. 12\", \"§ 1016.5(a)\", \"$5,000.00\", \"U.S.C.\"\nand \"Cf.\" are all terminators that end nothing, and over-splitting would\ntruncate a requirement away from its own condition.\n\nextraction/candidates.py is the contract any extractor must satisfy,\nmodel-driven or not, built around three controls that each close a\ndifferent route for unsupported content:\n\n- Citations are restricted to spans the application offered, so a proposal\n  cannot cite text it was never shown.\n- A candidate has no ID field; identity is derived from the document hash,\n  the cited spans and the normalised kind, so reordering a batch cannot\n  change it and duplicates collapse by construction.\n- A field cannot be both asserted and declared absent. Declaring\n  `condition` absent while citing a condition span stays legal, because\n  \"there is condition text here\" and \"I did not type it\" are both true of\n  an untyped extractor and the IR must be able to say exactly that.\n\nextraction/deterministic.py finds normative sentences and emits clauses\nthat assert almost nothing: a modality, the text, and where the text is.\nIt types no condition, effect or threshold, so its output is graph-only by\nconstruction and can never reach DMN or BPMN. That is the point — it gives\nthe pipeline a real input path over the real corpus today and is the\nbaseline a model-driven extractor has to beat. Role carving recognises a\nleading condition and a trailing qualifier, the two drafting shapes with\nunambiguous boundaries, and cites anything else whole rather than\nmis-splitting it.\n\nFixes a real bug in merged code, found by running the new extractor over\nrealistic prose: the modality markers were matched as plain substrings, so\n\"must not\" matched inside \"must notify\" and a routine obligation was read\nas a prohibition — and because negated forms are stripped before the\npositive markers are tested, its obligation reading was removed too. The\nsame flaw made \"can\" match inside \"candidate\" and \"may\" inside \"maybe\".\nMarkers now match whole words only. Twelve cases pinned, including\n\"must notarize\", \"Each candidate must submit\" and \"Maybe the shallow\nreview suffices\".\n\nOne caveat stated in the docs and asserted in a test: the deterministic\nextractor reads modality from the same table the gate checks it against,\nso that check is vacuous for its output and informative only for a\nmodality proposed independently. A green gate over self-consistent output\nlooks identical to a green gate over validated output, so the distinction\nis written down rather than left to be discovered.\n\n30 new tests (462 total, 90% coverage), and `--extract` wired into the CLI.\nNothing outside apps/pipeline-p2c is touched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline-p2c): read modality from the regions the gate actually checks\n\nRunning the whole corpus surfaced 216 refusals that my own docs claimed\nwere impossible. The claim was that the gate''s modality attestation is\nvacuous for this extractor because both read the same marker table. They\ndid — but not the same *regions*. The extractor read the whole sentence\nwhile the gate reads only the subject, condition and effect spans, so a\nsentence whose only modal marker sat inside its \"unless\" clause was\nlabelled from text the gate does not treat as modal-bearing.\n\nEvery one of the 216 was that shape, and in each the gate was right. One\nexample: effect \"This clause generally excludes loss caused by a director\",\nexception \"unless the director is also a salaried employee. ''Dishonest\nacts'' are defined as ...\" — the definition marker is in the exception, so\nthe clause was labelled a definition when its effect is not one.\n\nDropping those sentences would be wrong, because the requirement inside the\nexception is real. The fix is to derive modality from the modal-bearing\nregions, and when that yields nothing, treat the carve as mistaken and cite\nthe sentence whole so the marker falls inside a region the gate reads. No\nclause is lost: the two documents that produced refusals still yield 763\nand 822 clauses, now with zero blockers.\n\nWhole-corpus run after the fix: 17 PDFs, 5,633 pages, 18.5M characters in\n408s, giving 4,350 chunks, 38,520 spans and 29,343 clauses — 29,343 graph\nrules, 0 DMN, 0 BPMN, 0 blockers. Coverage accounts for every chunk,\nincluding the 21 image-only pages.\n\nTwo regression tests: the exact exception-only shape, and a property test\nasserting that whatever the carve, a declared modality is attested by the\nmodal-bearing roles. The caveat in the docs is corrected to say the check\ncannot fail because the regions are aligned, rather than asserting vacuity\nthat did not hold.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Potential fix for pull request finding\n\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): make a fabricated citation unexpressible, not just refused (#99)\n\nAdds the request/response seam a model-driven extractor plugs into. No\nmodel, provider or agent framework is committed to — the application emits\nrequests and consumes replies, so wiring any caller later is a small step.\n\nThe design point is where the integrity control sits. The earlier contract\nhad a proposal supply span IDs and checked them against an offer set, which\nworks but catches a fabrication only after it has been produced, and reports\nit as \"unknown evidence\" rather than \"this extractor cited text it never\nsaw\".\n\nA proposal now cites numbered *unit indices*. extraction/offer.py splits a\nchunk into sentence units with absolute offsets; extraction/contract.py\ngenerates a JSON Schema whose index fields enumerate exactly the units that\nrequest offered, so a structured-output API cannot emit anything else —\n\"99 is not one of [0, 1]\" comes from the schema, before any parsing.\nextraction/proposals.py resolves an index against the request and the\napplication builds the evidence span itself, so an admitted clause''s\nprovenance is always something the application computed.\n\nTwo consequences follow from the same reasoning. display_text comes from the\ncited unit rather than free text, so it cannot drift from the source it\nclaims to paraphrase. And a request deliberately excludes the document''s\nfull text: an extractor able to read past its units could reason about\nmaterial it has no way to cite, producing a clause whose evidence does not\ncover its own claim.\n\nThe schema and the prose contract are both generated from the definitions\nthe parsers already use, via newly exposed accessors on policy_ir.jsonschema\nrather than duplicated fragments. A hand-written prompt drifts from the code\nvalidating its output, and the drift always favours the looser side.\n\nCLI: --emit-requests DIR writes request, schema and instructions per chunk;\n--proposals FILE... admits replies. Ingestion is deterministic, so\nre-ingesting rebuilds byte-identical requests and the indices still resolve.\nVerified end to end on a real bulletin: emit, answer, admit, and the clause\nreaches the graph with reference_verified true and dmn_eligible false.\n\nAlso adds extraction/ to the domain-agnostic guard, which it was missing.\n\n29 new tests (493 total).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"4d2288a176353a684947c6be9aa12e99b9b13a03","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/4d2288a176353a684947c6be9aa12e99b9b13a03"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/918c841a0d9d204ce65f2461dd076bb0e368a436","verification":{"payload":"tree 4d2288a176353a684947c6be9aa12e99b9b13a03\nparent e49d6b3e1cd53772955c01ad0ac265b8bd722146\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787163117 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787163117 -0700\n\nfeat(pipeline-p2c): add the extraction contract and a model-free baseline (#97)\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): add the extraction contract and a model-free baseline\n\nCloses the last deterministic gap: documents now reach the knowledge graph\nend to end without a model anywhere in the path.\n\nextraction/sentences.py segments canonical text while preserving absolute\noffsets, because those offsets are the provenance a clause will cite — a\nsegmenter returning strings would force a search to recover position, and\nboilerplate that recurs verbatim could then not be located at all.\nSplitting is conservative: \"Ch. 12\", \"§ 1016.5(a)\", \"$5,000.00\", \"U.S.C.\"\nand \"Cf.\" are all terminators that end nothing, and over-splitting would\ntruncate a requirement away from its own condition.\n\nextraction/candidates.py is the contract any extractor must satisfy,\nmodel-driven or not, built around three controls that each close a\ndifferent route for unsupported content:\n\n- Citations are restricted to spans the application offered, so a proposal\n  cannot cite text it was never shown.\n- A candidate has no ID field; identity is derived from the document hash,\n  the cited spans and the normalised kind, so reordering a batch cannot\n  change it and duplicates collapse by construction.\n- A field cannot be both asserted and declared absent. Declaring\n  `condition` absent while citing a condition span stays legal, because\n  \"there is condition text here\" and \"I did not type it\" are both true of\n  an untyped extractor and the IR must be able to say exactly that.\n\nextraction/deterministic.py finds normative sentences and emits clauses\nthat assert almost nothing: a modality, the text, and where the text is.\nIt types no condition, effect or threshold, so its output is graph-only by\nconstruction and can never reach DMN or BPMN. That is the point — it gives\nthe pipeline a real input path over the real corpus today and is the\nbaseline a model-driven extractor has to beat. Role carving recognises a\nleading condition and a trailing qualifier, the two drafting shapes with\nunambiguous boundaries, and cites anything else whole rather than\nmis-splitting it.\n\nFixes a real bug in merged code, found by running the new extractor over\nrealistic prose: the modality markers were matched as plain substrings, so\n\"must not\" matched inside \"must notify\" and a routine obligation was read\nas a prohibition — and because negated forms are stripped before the\npositive markers are tested, its obligation reading was removed too. The\nsame flaw made \"can\" match inside \"candidate\" and \"may\" inside \"maybe\".\nMarkers now match whole words only. Twelve cases pinned, including\n\"must notarize\", \"Each candidate must submit\" and \"Maybe the shallow\nreview suffices\".\n\nOne caveat stated in the docs and asserted in a test: the deterministic\nextractor reads modality from the same table the gate checks it against,\nso that check is vacuous for its output and informative only for a\nmodality proposed independently. A green gate over self-consistent output\nlooks identical to a green gate over validated output, so the distinction\nis written down rather than left to be discovered.\n\n30 new tests (462 total, 90% coverage), and `--extract` wired into the CLI.\nNothing outside apps/pipeline-p2c is touched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline-p2c): read modality from the regions the gate actually checks\n\nRunning the whole corpus surfaced 216 refusals that my own docs claimed\nwere impossible. The claim was that the gate''s modality attestation is\nvacuous for this extractor because both read the same marker table. They\ndid — but not the same *regions*. The extractor read the whole sentence\nwhile the gate reads only the subject, condition and effect spans, so a\nsentence whose only modal marker sat inside its \"unless\" clause was\nlabelled from text the gate does not treat as modal-bearing.\n\nEvery one of the 216 was that shape, and in each the gate was right. One\nexample: effect \"This clause generally excludes loss caused by a director\",\nexception \"unless the director is also a salaried employee. ''Dishonest\nacts'' are defined as ...\" — the definition marker is in the exception, so\nthe clause was labelled a definition when its effect is not one.\n\nDropping those sentences would be wrong, because the requirement inside the\nexception is real. The fix is to derive modality from the modal-bearing\nregions, and when that yields nothing, treat the carve as mistaken and cite\nthe sentence whole so the marker falls inside a region the gate reads. No\nclause is lost: the two documents that produced refusals still yield 763\nand 822 clauses, now with zero blockers.\n\nWhole-corpus run after the fix: 17 PDFs, 5,633 pages, 18.5M characters in\n408s, giving 4,350 chunks, 38,520 spans and 29,343 clauses — 29,343 graph\nrules, 0 DMN, 0 BPMN, 0 blockers. Coverage accounts for every chunk,\nincluding the 21 image-only pages.\n\nTwo regression tests: the exact exception-only shape, and a property test\nasserting that whatever the carve, a declared modality is attested by the\nmodal-bearing roles. The caveat in the docs is corrected to say the check\ncannot fail because the regions are aligned, rather than asserting vacuity\nthat did not hold.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Potential fix for pull request finding\n\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): make a fabricated citation unexpressible, not just refused (#99)\n\nAdds the request/response seam a model-driven extractor plugs into. No\nmodel, provider or agent framework is committed to — the application emits\nrequests and consumes replies, so wiring any caller later is a small step.\n\nThe design point is where the integrity control sits. The earlier contract\nhad a proposal supply span IDs and checked them against an offer set, which\nworks but catches a fabrication only after it has been produced, and reports\nit as \"unknown evidence\" rather than \"this extractor cited text it never\nsaw\".\n\nA proposal now cites numbered *unit indices*. extraction/offer.py splits a\nchunk into sentence units with absolute offsets; extraction/contract.py\ngenerates a JSON Schema whose index fields enumerate exactly the units that\nrequest offered, so a structured-output API cannot emit anything else —\n\"99 is not one of [0, 1]\" comes from the schema, before any parsing.\nextraction/proposals.py resolves an index against the request and the\napplication builds the evidence span itself, so an admitted clause''s\nprovenance is always something the application computed.\n\nTwo consequences follow from the same reasoning. display_text comes from the\ncited unit rather than free text, so it cannot drift from the source it\nclaims to paraphrase. And a request deliberately excludes the document''s\nfull text: an extractor able to read past its units could reason about\nmaterial it has no way to cite, producing a clause whose evidence does not\ncover its own claim.\n\nThe schema and the prose contract are both generated from the definitions\nthe parsers already use, via newly exposed accessors on policy_ir.jsonschema\nrather than duplicated fragments. A hand-written prompt drifts from the code\nvalidating its output, and the drift always favours the looser side.\n\nCLI: --emit-requests DIR writes request, schema and instructions per chunk;\n--proposals FILE... admits replies. Ingestion is deterministic, so\nre-ingesting rebuilds byte-identical requests and the indices still resolve.\nVerified end to end on a real bulletin: emit, answer, admit, and the clause\nreaches the graph with reference_verified true and dmn_eligible false.\n\nAlso adds extraction/ to the domain-agnostic guard, which it was missing.\n\n29 new tests (493 total).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhfHtCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAh2oQABqjKtqgNhouleidZYQ9mwbl\nJzzzDFY+PyYM+bQkJmFArik0Pc88v9ggIrNEhaXWa0+Hlb8i1j3tE6D8rdEtK9Qa\nFJwN2BCuSStZ1dvV4e/y357V85HeQbOKj+5kCeQN0V+fjKLgYsY3MQ0DTDh+Tvcv\n3Apw56DkL+ielrG/AURTpZl09BKvfs4J4uHj4rFPJb/7KmF3uaYpEKkImkW7FIST\nvC7dcfowc3Fcj31JLCm36gwO0HxpVrU9EpbOS++diS3MRRVHWqryAYfKJVRQ3VYW\nUBV3o+QSQ2arqoiIGHQttghBE6esJbO3g+PhW/rR751ujSZ0VNeOpW0k2CapWQzn\nrrKz5fV2Et9HPYWAquoiWf2mqfph4nn6sxVvL8CAQ7+1/JQAC7hcXHUt5PHd1x7w\nYPPvFcmHIneGCHoH7+qsgtkEk0gYUvSqLQ+JuwCnn2m4V0gsUaV5mXz6RaXCflFx\n/MrCXtiiXd/njRRcYcTlFVNovJ9avirMLmsbj2mBSeFWUdEPwu40JW1PhdRrpzNU\nQhRcxuhQVocIbarEW3VLNxPjVbx/DvmGr/3v+AgQxnFcTFnzVOtKz5N02Ch37z7n\nvcZbiRzd4j7PxnrYQgMJzYuusDtQAv8r5RC5jHTOuAFcnVy3j/xWzi+DoXHaCfKL\nf7aeXbHOu2T+9ncf+ppb\n=sjwx\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T18:11:58Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/918c841a0d9d204ce65f2461dd076bb0e368a436","node_id":"C_kwDOTG-nuNoAKDkxOGM4NDFhMGQ5ZDIwNGNlNjVmMjQ2MWRkMDc2YmIwZTM2OGE0MzY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/e49d6b3e1cd53772955c01ad0ac265b8bd722146","sha":"e49d6b3e1cd53772955c01ad0ac265b8bd722146","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/e49d6b3e1cd53772955c01ad0ac265b8bd722146"}],"sha":"918c841a0d9d204ce65f2461dd076bb0e368a436","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/918c841a0d9d204ce65f2461dd076bb0e368a436"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/145064767?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci","id":145064767,"login":"rrahimi-uci","node_id":"U_kgDOCKWDPw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/e49d6b3e1cd53772955c01ad0ac265b8bd722146/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T18:01:54Z","email":"145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Reza Rahimi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T18:01:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"perf(pipeline-p2c): make the gate and graph projection linear, not quadratic (#98)\n\nFound while running the corpus end to end: a run over the committed PDFs\nnever finished. The cause was per-item rebuilds of work that belongs to the\nwhole run, invisible in fixtures because both factors are small there.\n\nFour hot spots, in order of cost:\n\n- _verify_span re-hashed the entire chunk body to confirm its digest — once\n  per citation. On a 3,200-clause document that was 68% of total gate time,\n  hashing the same 60 KB body 3,200 times. A chunk''s integrity is a property\n  of the chunk, not of each span citing it, so it is computed once per run\n  and consulted per span. \"Cannot check\" and \"does not match\" stay distinct\n  verdicts.\n- The gate rebuilt the evidence, chunk and document indexes per span.\n- The graph projection rebuilt the evidence and document indexes per rule.\n- _check_clause rebuilt the clause-ID set, the section-path set and the FEEL\n  name map per clause.\n\nPolicyIR is a value — every field is a tuple — so a cached index cannot go\nstale. All twelve indexes are memoised, plus two new derived sets, in a\nplain attribute rather than a dataclass field so equality, hashing and\nto_dict() are unaffected. The FEEL name map is built once in run_gate and\npassed down.\n\nMeasured on a synthetic document whose clause and span counts grow together:\n\n  clauses    before            after\n      400    0.05s             0.00s\n      800    0.18s  (x3.7)     0.01s  (x1.9)\n     1600    0.72s  (x4.0)     0.02s  (x1.9)\n     3200    2.83s  (x3.9)     0.04s  (x2.0)\n\nQuadratic growth becomes linear, and the largest case is 70x faster.\n\nFive guards in tests/test_scaling.py, all counting operations rather than\ntiming so they cannot flake: an index must return the same object twice, one\nchunk must be hashed exactly once however many clauses cite it, hashed bytes\nmust not grow with the square of the input, the projection must not rebuild\nits index per rule, and the cache must not leak into equality or to_dict().\n\nNo behaviour change: all 432 existing tests pass unchanged.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8c64002f6c539211808ad6d52b95bac17c367b5d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/8c64002f6c539211808ad6d52b95bac17c367b5d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/e49d6b3e1cd53772955c01ad0ac265b8bd722146","verification":{"payload":"tree 8c64002f6c539211808ad6d52b95bac17c367b5d\nparent 00facf4aefaa4abfbf145c2a9f630129d2add40a\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787162514 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787162514 -0700\n\nperf(pipeline-p2c): make the gate and graph projection linear, not quadratic (#98)\n\nFound while running the corpus end to end: a run over the committed PDFs\nnever finished. The cause was per-item rebuilds of work that belongs to the\nwhole run, invisible in fixtures because both factors are small there.\n\nFour hot spots, in order of cost:\n\n- _verify_span re-hashed the entire chunk body to confirm its digest — once\n  per citation. On a 3,200-clause document that was 68% of total gate time,\n  hashing the same 60 KB body 3,200 times. A chunk''s integrity is a property\n  of the chunk, not of each span citing it, so it is computed once per run\n  and consulted per span. \"Cannot check\" and \"does not match\" stay distinct\n  verdicts.\n- The gate rebuilt the evidence, chunk and document indexes per span.\n- The graph projection rebuilt the evidence and document indexes per rule.\n- _check_clause rebuilt the clause-ID set, the section-path set and the FEEL\n  name map per clause.\n\nPolicyIR is a value — every field is a tuple — so a cached index cannot go\nstale. All twelve indexes are memoised, plus two new derived sets, in a\nplain attribute rather than a dataclass field so equality, hashing and\nto_dict() are unaffected. The FEEL name map is built once in run_gate and\npassed down.\n\nMeasured on a synthetic document whose clause and span counts grow together:\n\n  clauses    before            after\n      400    0.05s             0.00s\n      800    0.18s  (x3.7)     0.01s  (x1.9)\n     1600    0.72s  (x4.0)     0.02s  (x1.9)\n     3200    2.83s  (x3.9)     0.04s  (x2.0)\n\nQuadratic growth becomes linear, and the largest case is 70x faster.\n\nFive guards in tests/test_scaling.py, all counting operations rather than\ntiming so they cannot flake: an index must return the same object twice, one\nchunk must be hashed exactly once however many clauses cite it, hashed bytes\nmust not grow with the square of the input, the projection must not rebuild\nits index per rule, and the cache must not leak into equality or to_dict().\n\nNo behaviour change: all 432 existing tests pass unchanged.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhe+SCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAASeAQAFYa8GW+CIGBIba3fDm7AGPA\nGQQpRVyb1UL47/eYbARw/C0Beb4jguXwHsjRt82xqajO4O6etTUaCAyrAsObetwI\nwEY6E/xsgV30PV0WxMcxKK25ekNNgNJjc2mVHRiMuCf4TBsozu05nCOq+4v9GGYP\nK1XvAUXiByBs9Qa6IS4a48ZamPRYnhWc+Zjly4e5O3RJKmGcU85n1Zes/zFs6D/e\nYVrhW+H2S9SnwpMcHpBNqwTNcDfgwo7YV3pWUJJ1COpyj4F+Q7nxC4d40XVb0qUh\nsJR9835g6kzao2++l1Fw/GkY/M+E6ee7jkH4KsDFxPBSaZITjur4zR7OJthfgj3P\nAcA1W72Udi+8BtoqM1d7m2dpki7iuUpeO840AO92OcvnpbCv4+/9HXb5qpiB5K2j\nJrfVUTEJ+nPTPGGJuhksXpqifPpqG+HmaTnaiCtrCoulQwToEaNyE4MEfwAP1por\nk58WdXrvDA5xs8TNCxwwBV+W/SJF5P0H8dq2b4qjghwd9nNNwhWc2MV72qO6u1oL\nv9vbw0imAAJUY1xKrXn4lX8KmngGhGnCd/bW58B5RpVQK9NAtQqhz/bjHS60Yvye\ndfMk83zt4Jc7syQE447u0u7mk90Zr8cxYIXOCXHNXwyEHb8v2wOLE3Z7gZFPn2JA\nC/Ph7QDA9aFew8xIWQcX\n=BWmN\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T18:01:55Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-fl