eat(pipeline-p2c): ingest PDFs into hashed canonical text with real offsets (#96)\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): add contextual scope, authority precedence and supersession\n\nCloses the three expressiveness gaps that decide whether the Policy IR\ngeneralises across regulated industries or bends toward whichever domain\ngets built out first. Schema-layer only, entirely offline, and confined to\napps/pipeline-p2c.\n\nScope. jurisdiction_scope was a tuple of strings, which read fine and did\nnothing: the non-overlap prover only sees the condition AST, so two state\noverlays with different thresholds were reported as overlapping and\nneither compiled. Scope is now a list of *named axes* whose vocabularies\nare declared as ScopeDimensionDefinition records — fixed fields would\nhard-code one domain, since mortgage scoping uses state/product/channel\nwhile healthcare uses payer/facility/state. Each axis becomes an ordinary\ninput column in the emitted decision table, so the existing prover\nseparates US-CA from US-NY with no special case, and a 660 California rule\nnow compiles alongside a 640 New York rule. Axes are derived from the rows\nrather than declared on the decision, so a table''s shape cannot drift out\nof step with the clauses it is built from.\n\nAuthority. Precedence between a statute, a guide and a bulletin is a legal\njudgement, so AuthoritySource.authority_weight is declared per corpus and\nthe engine only compares weights; kind stays a free string because a\nclosed enum would encode one industry''s vocabulary. A declared conflict\nnow has three outcomes instead of one: no real conflict when the scopes\nare provably disjoint, resolved when one authority is heavier, and\nunresolved on an equal weight or a missing authority. Previously any\nconflict killed the whole decision; a resolved conflict now refuses only\nthe losing clause — which stays in the graph — and the decision compiles\nfrom the winner. _ROW_EXCLUDED_BY_DESIGN names the three reasons a row is\nlegitimately absent so that dropping one does not block its table.\n\nSupersession and time. Lifecycle.SUPERSEDED recorded a status without\nrecording the replacement, making \"what applied on 3 March 2026\"\nunanswerable, and treating it as a veto broke historical queries outright.\nSupersession is now a typed SUPERSEDES edge paired with the replacement''s\neffective period, and a replacement whose start date is still in the\nfuture does not yet displace what it supersedes. A new --as-of takes the\ndate as an explicit argument; nothing reads the clock, because output that\ndepends on when it ran cannot be byte-stable.\n\nDomain-agnostic enforcement. Found and fixed a real leak: a hard-coded\n\"no lender may\" prohibition marker inside the engine, now a generic\nno <actor> may|shall|can pattern that covers every industry. A new test\nwalks the engine''s AST and fails on any domain noun appearing in code\nrather than prose, and it was verified to fail when the leak is\nreintroduced. Rewriting the marker scan also fixed a latent bug: \"no\ninsurer shall deny\" previously attested an obligation via its own\nsubstring.\n\nAlso: 7 new conformance fixtures (21 total), 66 new tests (401 total, 89%\ncoverage), the JSON Schema field overrides qualified by record name so two\nrecords can use the same field name for different things, and schema\nversion bumped to policy-ir-2.1.0.\n\nNothing outside apps/pipeline-p2c is touched, so the existing pipeline is\nunaffected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline-p2c): address review on scope projection and tie detection\n\nTwo findings from the review, both real.\n\nA negated jurisdiction was flattened into the legacy `jurisdiction` field,\nwhich legacy consumers read as an allow-list — so \"everywhere except\nCalifornia\" was emitted as [\"US-CA\"] and read as exactly the opposite.\nNegated constraints are now omitted from that flat field and carried\nexactly in `applicability_scope` and `scope`, where the negation survives.\n\nAUTHORITY_TIE was detected by substring-matching \"equal weight\" in a\nhuman-readable message, so rephrasing the message would have silently\nstopped reporting ties. ConflictOutcome now carries an `unresolved_code`\nwith named constants and an `is_tie` property computed from the authority\nweights themselves.\n\nFour regression tests added, one per finding plus their positive\ncounterparts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): ingest PDFs into hashed canonical text with real offsets\n\nAdds Stage 0/1: the missing producer of Policy IR from actual documents.\nUntil now the compiler could only consume hand-written fixtures or import\nlegacy graphs, so it was a well-tested island. `--ingest` turns the\ncommitted compliance PDFs into a Policy IR skeleton — hashed canonical\ntext, section-aligned chunks with real character offsets, and a coverage\nledger — which is what clause extraction consumes next. No model is\ninvolved; the whole stage is deterministic.\n\nThree decisions were forced by what the real corpus does.\n\nCanonical text is the whitespace-normalised extraction, not the raw\noutput. Extractors wrap lines mid-sentence, so \"the Lender must pay the\nfee within 10 business days\" does not occur in the raw text as a\ncontiguous string and a sentence-level citation cannot be anchored at all.\nNormalising at ingestion and hashing that makes offsets stable and\ncitations readable; the raw bytes are still hashed separately, and\nparser_version records the transformation (pypdf-6.16.1+normalize-1),\nbecause a different extractor is a different canonical text.\n\nSection detection has the opposite requirement — headings anchor at line\nstarts, which normalisation destroys. Rather than carry two texts with two\ncoordinate systems, canonicalise() builds the canonical text and\ntranslates a caller-supplied set of raw offsets in the same pass. The\ncaller passes the offsets it needs because a full index over the largest\ndocument in the corpus would be three million entries to answer eighty\nquestions. The heading patterns are drafting conventions, not domain\nterms: § 1016.5(a), Section 4.2, Chapter 3, Ch. 12, Part II, Appendix V,\nSubpart C3, 7.1 — and a mid-sentence \"see § 1016.8\" is correctly not a\nheading.\n\nPages that yield no text are recorded, not skipped. 21 pages across the\ncorpus are scanned images. Each becomes a zero-length chunk carrying the\npage number and extraction_failed in the coverage ledger, because emitting\nnothing would make a 467-page document with 20 scanned pages look fully\nprocessed. OCR is deliberately absent: its output is not deterministic\nacross versions, which would break the byte-stability everything else\nrests on.\n\npypdf is the one optional dependency, imported lazily by ingestion/pdf.py\nalone with a clear message when absent. Everything downstream of ingestion\nstays dependency-free.\n\n25 new tests (426 total, 89% coverage), run against the real committed\ncorpus and skipping cleanly without it. They read apps/pipeline''s\ncompliance-files and never write there; no code outside apps/pipeline-p2c\nis touched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline-p2c): address review on placeholder ids, dead code and test skips\n\nThree findings from the review, all real. The first was a genuine bug that\nmy own tests were too weak to catch.\n\nPlaceholder chunk IDs collided. Every page with no extractable text\nproduced a chunk hashed from the same empty content at the same offset, so\nall of them derived one identical ID and later placeholders overwrote\nearlier ones in the registry. Reproduced on the SBA SOP: 20 unextractable\npages collapsed to a single chunk and only page 464 survived, with 20\ncoverage entries all pointing at it. The placeholder ID is now derived\nfrom the page number via ids.missing_page_chunk_id, behind a purpose-named\nSourceRegistry.add_page_placeholder so the special case cannot be misused\nfor real chunks. All 20 pages are now preserved.\n\nTwo tests let this through and are fixed: the coverage-ledger test\ncompared sets, which hides duplicate entries pointing at one chunk, and now\ncompares sorted lists and asserts chunk-ID uniqueness; and a new test\ningests the 467-page SOP specifically to assert one placeholder per missing\npage, distinct IDs, and page numbers preserved through the registry.\n\nThe module-level importorskip(\"pypdf\") skipped the whole file, including\nthe section-detection and canonicalisation tests that never open a PDF.\nOnly the PDF-dependent tests are skipped now. Verified in a venv without\nthe extra: 9 tests run and pass where previously all 27 were skipped.\n\n_LINE_BREAKS was dead code — normalisation is done character by character\ninside canonicalise — and is removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5ccccca7e7a05b3d4bd32a731a4b77d4a9cd4fc3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/5ccccca7e7a05b3d4bd32a731a4b77d4a9cd4fc3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/321a7198fd17ccca6e835895a560e62ce02b8dd6","verification":{"payload":"tree 5ccccca7e7a05b3d4bd32a731a4b77d4a9cd4fc3\nparent f78abf8511f065fa6706ae6dafec26466f3679a9\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787160253 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787160253 -0700\n\nfeat(pipeline-p2c): ingest PDFs into hashed canonical text with real offsets (#96)\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): add contextual scope, authority precedence and supersession\n\nCloses the three expressiveness gaps that decide whether the Policy IR\ngeneralises across regulated industries or bends toward whichever domain\ngets built out first. Schema-layer only, entirely offline, and confined to\napps/pipeline-p2c.\n\nScope. jurisdiction_scope was a tuple of strings, which read fine and did\nnothing: the non-overlap prover only sees the condition AST, so two state\noverlays with different thresholds were reported as overlapping and\nneither compiled. Scope is now a list of *named axes* whose vocabularies\nare declared as ScopeDimensionDefinition records — fixed fields would\nhard-code one domain, since mortgage scoping uses state/product/channel\nwhile healthcare uses payer/facility/state. Each axis becomes an ordinary\ninput column in the emitted decision table, so the existing prover\nseparates US-CA from US-NY with no special case, and a 660 California rule\nnow compiles alongside a 640 New York rule. Axes are derived from the rows\nrather than declared on the decision, so a table''s shape cannot drift out\nof step with the clauses it is built from.\n\nAuthority. Precedence between a statute, a guide and a bulletin is a legal\njudgement, so AuthoritySource.authority_weight is declared per corpus and\nthe engine only compares weights; kind stays a free string because a\nclosed enum would encode one industry''s vocabulary. A declared conflict\nnow has three outcomes instead of one: no real conflict when the scopes\nare provably disjoint, resolved when one authority is heavier, and\nunresolved on an equal weight or a missing authority. Previously any\nconflict killed the whole decision; a resolved conflict now refuses only\nthe losing clause — which stays in the graph — and the decision compiles\nfrom the winner. _ROW_EXCLUDED_BY_DESIGN names the three reasons a row is\nlegitimately absent so that dropping one does not block its table.\n\nSupersession and time. Lifecycle.SUPERSEDED recorded a status without\nrecording the replacement, making \"what applied on 3 March 2026\"\nunanswerable, and treating it as a veto broke historical queries outright.\nSupersession is now a typed SUPERSEDES edge paired with the replacement''s\neffective period, and a replacement whose start date is still in the\nfuture does not yet displace what it supersedes. A new --as-of takes the\ndate as an explicit argument; nothing reads the clock, because output that\ndepends on when it ran cannot be byte-stable.\n\nDomain-agnostic enforcement. Found and fixed a real leak: a hard-coded\n\"no lender may\" prohibition marker inside the engine, now a generic\nno <actor> may|shall|can pattern that covers every industry. A new test\nwalks the engine''s AST and fails on any domain noun appearing in code\nrather than prose, and it was verified to fail when the leak is\nreintroduced. Rewriting the marker scan also fixed a latent bug: \"no\ninsurer shall deny\" previously attested an obligation via its own\nsubstring.\n\nAlso: 7 new conformance fixtures (21 total), 66 new tests (401 total, 89%\ncoverage), the JSON Schema field overrides qualified by record name so two\nrecords can use the same field name for different things, and schema\nversion bumped to policy-ir-2.1.0.\n\nNothing outside apps/pipeline-p2c is touched, so the existing pipeline is\nunaffected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline-p2c): address review on scope projection and tie detection\n\nTwo findings from the review, both real.\n\nA negated jurisdiction was flattened into the legacy `jurisdiction` field,\nwhich legacy consumers read as an allow-list — so \"everywhere except\nCalifornia\" was emitted as [\"US-CA\"] and read as exactly the opposite.\nNegated constraints are now omitted from that flat field and carried\nexactly in `applicability_scope` and `scope`, where the negation survives.\n\nAUTHORITY_TIE was detected by substring-matching \"equal weight\" in a\nhuman-readable message, so rephrasing the message would have silently\nstopped reporting ties. ConflictOutcome now carries an `unresolved_code`\nwith named constants and an `is_tie` property computed from the authority\nweights themselves.\n\nFour regression tests added, one per finding plus their positive\ncounterparts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): ingest PDFs into hashed canonical text with real offsets\n\nAdds Stage 0/1: the missing producer of Policy IR from actual documents.\nUntil now the compiler could only consume hand-written fixtures or import\nlegacy graphs, so it was a well-tested island. `--ingest` turns the\ncommitted compliance PDFs into a Policy IR skeleton — hashed canonical\ntext, section-aligned chunks with real character offsets, and a coverage\nledger — which is what clause extraction consumes next. No model is\ninvolved; the whole stage is deterministic.\n\nThree decisions were forced by what the real corpus does.\n\nCanonical text is the whitespace-normalised extraction, not the raw\noutput. Extractors wrap lines mid-sentence, so \"the Lender must pay the\nfee within 10 business days\" does not occur in the raw text as a\ncontiguous string and a sentence-level citation cannot be anchored at all.\nNormalising at ingestion and hashing that makes offsets stable and\ncitations readable; the raw bytes are still hashed separately, and\nparser_version records the transformation (pypdf-6.16.1+normalize-1),\nbecause a different extractor is a different canonical text.\n\nSection detection has the opposite requirement — headings anchor at line\nstarts, which normalisation destroys. Rather than carry two texts with two\ncoordinate systems, canonicalise() builds the canonical text and\ntranslates a caller-supplied set of raw offsets in the same pass. The\ncaller passes the offsets it needs because a full index over the largest\ndocument in the corpus would be three million entries to answer eighty\nquestions. The heading patterns are drafting conventions, not domain\nterms: § 1016.5(a), Section 4.2, Chapter 3, Ch. 12, Part II, Appendix V,\nSubpart C3, 7.1 — and a mid-sentence \"see § 1016.8\" is correctly not a\nheading.\n\nPages that yield no text are recorded, not skipped. 21 pages across the\ncorpus are scanned images. Each becomes a zero-length chunk carrying the\npage number and extraction_failed in the coverage ledger, because emitting\nnothing would make a 467-page document with 20 scanned pages look fully\nprocessed. OCR is deliberately absent: its output is not deterministic\nacross versions, which would break the byte-stability everything else\nrests on.\n\npypdf is the one optional dependency, imported lazily by ingestion/pdf.py\nalone with a clear message when absent. Everything downstream of ingestion\nstays dependency-free.\n\n25 new tests (426 total, 89% coverage), run against the real committed\ncorpus and skipping cleanly without it. They read apps/pipeline''s\ncompliance-files and never write there; no code outside apps/pipeline-p2c\nis touched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(pipeline-p2c): address review on placeholder ids, dead code and test skips\n\nThree findings from the review, all real. The first was a genuine bug that\nmy own tests were too weak to catch.\n\nPlaceholder chunk IDs collided. Every page with no extractable text\nproduced a chunk hashed from the same empty content at the same offset, so\nall of them derived one identical ID and later placeholders overwrote\nearlier ones in the registry. Reproduced on the SBA SOP: 20 unextractable\npages collapsed to a single chunk and only page 464 survived, with 20\ncoverage entries all pointing at it. The placeholder ID is now derived\nfrom the page number via ids.missing_page_chunk_id, behind a purpose-named\nSourceRegistry.add_page_placeholder so the special case cannot be misused\nfor real chunks. All 20 pages are now preserved.\n\nTwo tests let this through and are fixed: the coverage-ledger test\ncompared sets, which hides duplicate entries pointing at one chunk, and now\ncompares sorted lists and asserts chunk-ID uniqueness; and a new test\ningests the 467-page SOP specifically to assert one placeholder per missing\npage, distinct IDs, and page numbers preserved through the registry.\n\nThe module-level importorskip(\"pypdf\") skipped the whole file, including\nthe section-detection and canonicalisation tests that never open a PDF.\nOnly the PDF-dependent tests are skipped now. Verified in a venv without\nthe extra: 9 tests run and pass where previously all 27 were skipped.\n\n_LINE_BREAKS was dead code — normalisation is done character by character\ninside canonicalise — and is removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhea9CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAws8QAIyl45htJDQajVXelgz0zw4K\nDxsiUAA2ucCvbKEwRJ+pIHoX5PH9wHPJ88enxelGGrU34MkIgAo4Co6donK5ZIwl\nVjpKslwyh1AfkhnjJsA/cRT6Br2yzYslgjzw+bUv7Y2jUP2u/HjaSpGJuitz856n\nYgFc8Cplt4CeivDojfpJmm04N2N3WhBqp+MHiBxeZikkZLIUiC3MStB1HJZckjWX\nu4dgvUj3rFsJmTXPtvj1IL0B65PDGjszsB35VNbg2SgZLH+LrHZZR/KtBqyx5u8N\nlvMdONmHgvxoS+sO+eg31jLNYjB6BQNbjSp+xRekIBWPuj2U7g9NSh9axKCjDtpG\nwqjt3ZjqlMJm7MHKIREKo62PXgB/yFaT9JMiRv3y3vuKHrnV3Voq8AxuIh5VBSc/\nnB93jHKw8y7c6M52QPGpImkk/bF9rmtLZfQP+qPLj+AS+QOCnhH36i9amGKAdybQ\n3ZRBxjkBgwZEn4FgPZjxl+bJgaN+Lh9pXxQ3gmv7hA0S/oLZfqySpHjQp+g147i9\nB88TkldgpxVNwnJhVLCCYatOtPRjOraklsSsDKA5ISexe+kORk1U/4TESIjkkQge\nppzaZReGQrIuCmvOfJhFmK9x7NQFThwV2KVLQI1dlv8xPzgXY2XpTiZN+WXdH2CL\nfZDHHhuwGWhuvIXLMNvr\n=9Qm/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T17:24:13Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/321a7198fd17ccca6e835895a560e62ce02b8dd6","node_id":"C_kwDOTG-nuNoAKDMyMWE3MTk4ZmQxN2NjY2E2ZTgzNTg5NWE1NjBlNjJjZTAyYjhkZDY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commit/f78abf8511f065fa6706ae6dafec26466f3679a9","sha":"f78abf8511f065fa6706ae6dafec26466f3679a9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/f78abf8511f065fa6706ae6dafec26466f3679a9"}],"sha":"321a7198fd17ccca6e835895a560e62ce02b8dd6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/321a7198fd17ccca6e835895a560e62ce02b8dd6"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/145064767?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/rrahimi-uci","id":145064767,"login":"rrahimi-uci","node_id":"U_kgDOCKWDPw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrahimi-uci","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/commits/f78abf8511f065fa6706ae6dafec26466f3679a9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T17:01:01Z","email":"145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Reza Rahimi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T17:01:01Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(pipeline-p2c): add contextual scope, authority precedence and supersession (#95)\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): add contextual scope, authority precedence and supersession\n\nCloses the three expressiveness gaps that decide whether the Policy IR\ngeneralises across regulated industries or bends toward whichever domain\ngets built out first. Schema-layer only, entirely offline, and confined to\napps/pipeline-p2c.\n\nScope. jurisdiction_scope was a tuple of strings, which read fine and did\nnothing: the non-overlap prover only sees the condition AST, so two state\noverlays with different thresholds were reported as overlapping and\nneither compiled. Scope is now a list of *named axes* whose vocabularies\nare declared as ScopeDimensionDefinition records — fixed fields would\nhard-code one domain, since mortgage scoping uses state/product/channel\nwhile healthcare uses payer/facility/state. Each axis becomes an ordinary\ninput column in the emitted decision table, so the existing prover\nseparates US-CA from US-NY with no special case, and a 660 California rule\nnow compiles alongside a 640 New York rule. Axes are derived from the rows\nrather than declared on the decision, so a table''s shape cannot drift out\nof step with the clauses it is built from.\n\nAuthority. Precedence between a statute, a guide and a bulletin is a legal\njudgement, so AuthoritySource.authority_weight is declared per corpus and\nthe engine only compares weights; kind stays a free string because a\nclosed enum would encode one industry''s vocabulary. A declared conflict\nnow has three outcomes instead of one: no real conflict when the scopes\nare provably disjoint, resolved when one authority is heavier, and\nunresolved on an equal weight or a missing authority. Previously any\nconflict killed the whole decision; a resolved conflict now refuses only\nthe losing clause — which stays in the graph — and the decision compiles\nfrom the winner. _ROW_EXCLUDED_BY_DESIGN names the three reasons a row is\nlegitimately absent so that dropping one does not block its table.\n\nSupersession and time. Lifecycle.SUPERSEDED recorded a status without\nrecording the replacement, making \"what applied on 3 March 2026\"\nunanswerable, and treating it as a veto broke historical queries outright.\nSupersession is now a typed SUPERSEDES edge paired with the replacement''s\neffective period, and a replacement whose start date is still in the\nfuture does not yet displace what it supersedes. A new --as-of takes the\ndate as an explicit argument; nothing reads the clock, because output that\ndepends on when it ran cannot be byte-stable.\n\nDomain-agnostic enforcement. Found and fixed a real leak: a hard-coded\n\"no lender may\" prohibition marker inside the engine, now a generic\nno <actor> may|shall|can pattern that covers every industry. A new test\nwalks the engine''s AST and fails on any domain noun appearing in code\nrather than prose, and it was verified to fail when the leak is\nreintroduced. Rewriting the marker scan also fixed a latent bug: \"no\ninsurer shall deny\" previously attested an obligation via its own\nsubstring.\n\nAlso: 7 new conformance fixtures (21 total), 66 new tests (401 total, 89%\ncoverage), the JSON Schema field overrides qualified by record name so two\nrecords can use the same field name for different things, and schema\nversion bumped to policy-ir-2.1.0.\n\nNothing outside apps/pipeline-p2c is touched, so the existing pipeline is\nunaffected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Potential fix for pull request finding\n\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Potential fix for pull request finding\n\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"c1dc6d4df58fa1b09021dcdb23e339ae600526b8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/trees/c1dc6d4df58fa1b09021dcdb23e339ae600526b8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrahimi-uci/policy-to-knowledge/git/commits/f78abf8511f065fa6706ae6dafec26466f3679a9","verification":{"payload":"tree c1dc6d4df58fa1b09021dcdb23e339ae600526b8\nparent 0fd13ff4e887b771b31d4010d81de0dc44c600fe\nauthor Reza Rahimi <145064767+rrahimi-uci@users.noreply.github.com> 1787158861 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787158861 -0700\n\nfeat(pipeline-p2c): add contextual scope, authority precedence and supersession (#95)\n\n* feat(pipeline-p2c): add contextual scope, authority precedence and supersession\n\nCloses the three expressiveness gaps that decide whether the Policy IR\ngeneralises across regulated industries or bends toward whichever domain\ngets built out first. Schema-layer only, entirely offline, and confined to\napps/pipeline-p2c.\n\nScope. jurisdiction_scope was a tuple of strings, which read fine and did\nnothing: the non-overlap prover only sees the condition AST, so two state\noverlays with different thresholds were reported as overlapping and\nneither compiled. Scope is now a list of *named axes* whose vocabularies\nare declared as ScopeDimensionDefinition records — fixed fields would\nhard-code one domain, since mortgage scoping uses state/product/channel\nwhile healthcare uses payer/facility/state. Each axis becomes an ordinary\ninput column in the emitted decision table, so the existing prover\nseparates US-CA from US-NY with no special case, and a 660 California rule\nnow compiles alongside a 640 New York rule. Axes are derived from the rows\nrather than declared on the decision, so a table''s shape cannot drift out\nof step with the clauses it is built from.\n\nAuthority. Precedence between a statute, a guide and a bulletin is a legal\njudgement, so AuthoritySource.authority_weight is declared per corpus and\nthe engine only compares weights; kind stays a free string because a\nclosed enum would encode one industry''s vocabulary. A declared conflict\nnow has three outcomes instead of one: no real conflict when the scopes\nare provably disjoint, resolved when one authority is heavier, and\nunresolved on an equal weight or a missing authority. Previously any\nconflict killed the whole decision; a resolved conflict now refuses only\nthe losing clause — which stays in the graph — and the decision compiles\nfrom the winner. _ROW_EXCLUDED_BY_DESIGN names the three reasons a row is\nlegitimately absent so that dropping one does not block its table.\n\nSupersession and time. Lifecycle.SUPERSEDED recorded a status without\nrecording the replacement, making \"what applied on 3 March 2026\"\nunanswerable, and treating it as a veto broke historical queries outright.\nSupersession is now a typed SUPERSEDES edge paired with the replacement''s\neffective period, and a replacement whose start date is still in the\nfuture does not yet displace what it supersedes. A new --as-of takes the\ndate as an explicit argument; nothing reads the clock, because output that\ndepends on when it ran cannot be byte-stable.\n\nDomain-agnostic enforcement. Found and fixed a real leak: a hard-coded\n\"no lender may\" prohibition marker inside the engine, now a generic\nno <actor> may|shall|can pattern that covers every industry. A new test\nwalks the engine''s AST and fails on any domain noun appearing in code\nrather than prose, and it was verified to fail when the leak is\nreintroduced. Rewriting the marker scan also fixed a latent bug: \"no\ninsurer shall deny\" previously attested an obligation via its own\nsubstring.\n\nAlso: 7 new conformance fixtures (21 total), 66 new tests (401 total, 89%\ncoverage), the JSON Schema field overrides qualified by record name so two\nrecords can use the same field name for different things, and schema\nversion bumped to policy-ir-2.1.0.\n\nNothing outside apps/pipeline-p2c is touched, so the existing pipeline is\nunaffected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Potential fix for pull request finding\n\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Potential fix for pull request finding\n\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqheFNCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA8gUQAAGjfO576WtaZYdP7QMT8jVF\nBdl/L7nuEInXDY9oE7f4l/r3ZeMV1HLavoYUzyJF1jNcpdEZsR0LlIZsd35ggZ3E\nR/JvdhdljxW6tnR0mZZ9G+C+hltNbg0TM7fGHkXXd92Fl4bL3Qb5SfVYhwfOti+n\nMMsOSmM6tx5EVSP8tKdjMETOMznkQgtBCq27YtnHkexU5IQ1+f0uY6DfPq1eRJVm\n8NWLjzCOXTRNTINEF3mqG8pfQFHlXVFmRC5A1yQ/KCc8Cg7ONedW4dRLq4aLZx5D\nAHBH6myz+bggFuPbJAsFupetuxb+jtD3DL2hD5nUfuKJbZWX+PTNMl4C6sWF78/E\nxnuYrb2YWkMB90t7++W5bJbl8wrYx4+wmyQitzR3fWljUXb3yJ9ODr9Y+3pk7WYp\n4xtE5pOgr8JrFcpzOiu5JtN2Y/5GjdlIyLbdW7AfNGHerK87WILy9aXMcCcvbWdr\n0DkYzcCgkzLA6Ycwr6tSUOK5bEq5oZUDYWjtPk8w/0e+UehJ1MGJz7tBqw0ZLKLb\nbJeyEYWUoQeZ7Y+DTwUWPs/7bHQ9eFF3ppLz6nz95tJh5LfExWeGpZ2BZIQ5icXZ\nELKfvPwNqaK+6lDFa9DvQveigagSBHMjBIf74Ufdg24sMDItpxDjuZZlWq+ma/Vi\nEB+37gD/QtvhD+IedXOx\n=5XqH\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T17:01: